window.BLK03EXAM = {"title": "Block 3 · Exam Prep", "passMark": 0.7, "mockSize": 60, "storeKey": "blk3-exam-v1", "figs": {"50": "data:image/png;base64,", "51": "data:image/png;base64,", "63": "data:image/png;base64,", "62": "data:image/png;base64,", "62A": "data:image/png;base64,", "62B": "data:image/png;base64,"}, "topics": [{"code": "FLUID", "title": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "ref": null, "qs": [{"id": "8192", "q": "Which coupling nut should be selected for use with 1/2-inch aluminum oil lines which are to be assembled using flared tube ends and standard AN nuts, sleeves, and fittings?", "options": ["AN-818-8.", "AN-818-5.", "AN-818-16."], "correct": 0, "short": "AN-818-8.", "expl": "The AN818 is the coupling (fitting) nut, and its dash number is the tube outside diameter measured in 1/16-inch steps. A 1/2-inch line is 8/16, so it takes an AN818-8. Read the dash number as sixteenths of an inch and you never mis-size a fitting.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-11", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8193", "q": "Metal tubing fluid lines are sized by wall thickness and", "options": ["inside diameter in 1/16 inch increments.", "outside diameter in 1/16 inch increments.", "outside diameter in 1/32 inch increments."], "correct": 1, "short": "outside diameter in 1/16 inch increments.", "expl": "Metal tubing is called out two ways at once: its wall thickness (how thick the metal is) and its outside diameter, and that OD is given in 1/16-inch increments. So a 'dash 8' tube is 8/16 = 1/2-inch OD. Inside diameter is what's left after you subtract both walls.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-2", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8193-1", "q": "Rolling-type flaring tools are used to flare __________, __________, and ___________ tubing.", "options": ["Stainless steel, hard copper, mild steel.", "Titanium, soft copper, corrosion resistant steel.", "Soft copper, aluminum, brass."], "correct": 2, "short": "Soft copper, aluminum, brass.", "expl": "Rolling-type flaring tools work the metal gently, so they're used on the soft, workable tubing: soft copper, aluminum, and brass. Hard or high-strength materials (stainless, steel, titanium) need the impact-type tool or a different process — a rolling tool would just distort them.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-4", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8194", "q": "Using the following steps, which sequence indicates the proper order the aviation mechanic would use to make a single flare on a piece of tubing? 1. Place the tube in the proper size hole in the flaring block. 2. Project the end of the tube slightly from the top of the flaring tool, about the thickness of a dime. 3. Slip the fitting nut and sleeve on the tube. 4. Strike the plunger several light blows with a lightweight hammer or mallet and turn the plunger one-half turn after each blow. 5. Tighten the clamp bar securely to prevent slippage. 6. Center the plunger or flaring pin over the tube.", "options": ["3, 1, 6, 2, 5, and 4.", "1, 3, 5, 2, 4, and 6.", "3, 1, 2, 6, 5, and 4."], "correct": 0, "short": "3, 1, 6, 2, 5, and 4.", "expl": "Order of a single flare: slip the fitting nut and sleeve on FIRST (you can't add them after the flare), set the tube in the correct hole of the flaring block, center the plunger, project the tube about a dime's thickness above the block, tighten the clamp so it can't slip, then strike the plunger — turning a half-turn between light blows. That sequence is 3, 1, 6, 2, 5, 4.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-4", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8195", "q": "Hydraulic tubing, which is damaged in a localized area to such an extent that repair is necessary, may be repaired", "options": ["only by replacing the tubing section run (connection to connection) using the same size and material as the original.", "by cutting out the damaged area and utilizing a swaged tube fitting to join the tube ends.", "by cutting out the damaged section and soldering in a replacement section of tubing."], "correct": 1, "short": "by cutting out the damaged area and utilizing a swaged tube fitting to join the tube ends.", "expl": "A local damage spot doesn't force a full connection-to-connection replacement. You may cut out the bad section and rejoin the tube ends with a swaged tube fitting (or an approved union), restoring the run without soldering, which has no place in a pressurized aircraft fluid line.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-14", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8196", "q": "What is an advantage of a double flare on aluminum tubing?", "options": ["Ease of construction.", "Can be applied to any size and wall-thickness of tubing.", "More resistant to damage when the joint is tightened."], "correct": 2, "short": "More resistant to damage when the joint is tightened.", "expl": "A double flare rolls the tube lip back on itself, giving two thicknesses of metal at the sealing face. That doubled, smoother, more concentric edge is far more resistant to being cut or cracked when the coupling nut is torqued down — its whole reason for existing on soft aluminum lines.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-5", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8197", "q": "A certain amount of slack must be left in a flexible hose during installation because, when under pressure, it", "options": ["contracts in length and expands in diameter.", "expands in length and diameter.", "expands in length and contracts in diameter."], "correct": 0, "short": "contracts in length and expands in diameter.", "expl": "Under pressure a flexible hose acts like a squeezed balloon in reverse: it swells in diameter and, as it fattens, it shortens in length. If you install it bar-tight it will try to pull its own end fittings loose, so you build in 5 to 8 percent slack to absorb that contraction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-21", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8198", "q": "The term “cold flow” is generally associated with", "options": ["the effects of low temperature gasses or liquids flowing in hose or tubing.", "impressions left in natural or synthetic rubber hose material.", "flexibility characteristics of various hose materials at low ambient temperatures."], "correct": 1, "short": "impressions left in natural or synthetic rubber hose material.", "expl": "'Cold flow' is the permanent impression a hose clamp or support leaves pressed into natural or synthetic rubber hose material over time — the rubber slowly 'flows' cold under steady load. Deep cold-flow marks are a sign of deterioration and a reason to replace the hose.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-18", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8199", "q": "What is the color of an AN steel flared-tube fitting?", "options": ["Blue.", "Red.", "Black."], "correct": 2, "short": "Black.", "expl": "All AN steel fittings are colored black; AN aluminum fittings are blue. So a black flared-tube fitting tells you at a glance it's steel — a quick material check before you ever touch a wrench.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-8", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8200", "q": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": ["AN fittings have an identifying shoulder between the end of the threads and the flare cone.", "AC and AN fittings are considered identical except for material composition and identifying colors.", "AN fittings are generally interchangeable with AC fittings of compatible material composition"], "correct": 0, "short": "AN fittings have an identifying shoulder between the end of the threads and the flare cone.", "expl": "The AN fitting has an identifying shoulder (a small step) between the end of the threads and the flare cone — the feature that distinguishes it from the older AC design. AN and AC fittings are NOT interchangeable; the shoulder and thread details differ, so mixing them is not acceptable.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-8", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8201", "q": "Flexible lines must be installed with", "options": ["a slack of at least 10 to 12 percent of the length.", "a slack of 5 to 8 percent of the length.", "enough slack to allow maximum flexing during operation."], "correct": 1, "short": "a slack of 5 to 8 percent of the length.", "expl": "Flexible lines are installed with a slack of 5 to 8 percent of the hose length. That built-in looseness lets the hose swell and shorten under pressure and lets the plumbing move with vibration without straining the end fittings.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-21", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8202", "q": "The maximum distance between end fittings to which a straight hose assembly is to be connected is 50 inches. The minimum hose length to make such a connection should be", "options": ["54-1/2 inches.", "51-1/2 inches.", "52-1/2 inches."], "correct": 2, "short": "52-1/2 inches.", "expl": "Provide 5 to 8 percent slack over the connected distance. Five percent of 50 inches is 2.5 inches, so the minimum hose length is 50 + 2.5 = 52-1/2 inches. Anything shorter would go bar-tight and pull on the fittings under pressure.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-21", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8203", "q": "Excessive stress on fluid or pneumatic metal tubing caused by expansion and contraction due to temperature changes can best be avoided by", "options": ["providing bends in the tubing.", "using short, straight sections of tubing between fixed parts of the aircraft.", "using tubing of the same material as the majority of the adjoining structure."], "correct": 0, "short": "providing bends in the tubing.", "expl": "Metal expands and contracts with temperature. A dead-straight rigid run has nowhere to move, so it builds up stress at the fittings. Deliberately putting bends in the tubing gives it a place to flex, relieving that expansion/contraction stress — the same reason you never install a straight rigid run between two fixed points.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-3", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8204", "q": "The material specifications for a certain aircraft require that a replacement oil line be fabricated from 3/4-inch 0.072 5052-0 aluminum alloy tubing. What the inside dimension of this tubing?", "options": ["0.688 inch.", "0.606 inch.", "0.750 inch."], "correct": 1, "short": "0.606 inch.", "expl": "Inside diameter = outside diameter minus both walls. For 3/4-inch (0.750) tube with a 0.072 wall: 0.750 - (2 x 0.072) = 0.750 - 0.144 = 0.606 inch. The '3/4-inch' size always names the OD, never the ID.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-2", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8204-1", "q": "The material specifications for a certain aircraft require that a replacement oil line be fabricated from 1/2 inch 0.072 5052-0 aluminum alloy tubing. What is the inside dimension of this tubing?", "options": ["0.428 inch.", "0.750 inch.", "0.356 inch."], "correct": 2, "short": "0.356 inch.", "expl": "Same subtraction: 1/2-inch (0.500) OD minus two 0.072 walls = 0.500 - 0.144 = 0.356 inch inside diameter. Remember the stated tube size (1/2 inch) is the OD; the ID is what fluid actually flows through.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-2", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8205", "q": "In most aircraft hydraulic systems, two-piece tube connectors consisting of a sleeve and a nut are used when a tubing flare is required. The use of this type connector eliminates", "options": ["the possibility of reducing the flare thickness by wiping or ironing during the tightening process.", "the flaring operation prior to assembly.", "wrench damage to the tubing during the tightening process."], "correct": 0, "short": "the possibility of reducing the flare thickness by wiping or ironing during the tightening process.", "expl": "The two-piece sleeve-and-nut connector lets the nut turn against the sleeve instead of directly against the flare. That protects the flare from being thinned by 'wiping' or 'ironing' — the metal-dragging that would occur if the nut bore down straight on the soft flare during tightening.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-5", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8206", "q": "During installation, Military Standard (MS) flareless fittings", "options": ["should be assembled by applying an anti-seize compound to both surfaces of the fitting and flare.", "are normally tightened by turning the nut a specified amount, unless the manufacturer lists torque values.", "are normally tightened by applying torque in small increments to prevent over-heating the tube mating surfaces."], "correct": 1, "short": "are normally tightened by turning the nut a specified amount, unless the manufacturer lists torque values.", "expl": "MS (flareless) fittings are normally set by turning the nut a specified amount past finger-tight, unless the manufacturer publishes torque values — then you use those. You do NOT slather anti-seize on the mating surfaces, and you don't tighten in tiny 'anti-overheat' increments; those are made-up procedures.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-8", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8207", "q": "When flaring aluminum tubing for use with AN fittings, the flare angle must be", "options": ["39°.", "45°.", "37°."], "correct": 2, "short": "37°.", "expl": "The AN standard flare angle for aluminum tubing is 37 degrees (the tool grinds a 35°-37° cone). Automotive-style 45° flares will not seal correctly against a 37° AN fitting, so matching the angle is critical.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-4", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8208", "q": "Scratches or nicks on the straight portion of aluminum alloy tubing may be repaired if they are no deeper than", "options": ["10 percent of the wall thickness.", "20 percent of the wall thickness.", "1/32 inch or 20 percent of wall thickness, whichever is less."], "correct": 0, "short": "10 percent of the wall thickness.", "expl": "On the straight portion of aluminum-alloy tubing, scratches or nicks may be smoothed out and returned to service as long as they are no deeper than 10 percent of the wall thickness. Deeper than that and the tube is weakened past repair.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-14", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8209", "q": "Flexible hose used in aircraft systems is classified in size according to the", "options": ["outside diameter.", "inside diameter.", "wall thickness."], "correct": 1, "short": "inside diameter.", "expl": "Flexible hose is classified (sized) by its inside diameter — the bore the fluid actually flows through. That's the opposite of rigid metal tubing, which is sized by outside diameter, so watch which kind of line the question is asking about.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-16", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8209-1", "q": "When a Teflon hose has been in service for time, what condition may have occurred and/or what precaution should be taken when it is temporarily removed from the aircraft?", "options": ["The hose interior must be kept wet with the fluid carried to prevent embrittlement/deterioration.", "The hose may become stiff and brittle if not flexed or moved regularly.", "The hose may have developed a set, or have been manufactured with a pre-set shape, and must be supported to maintain its shape."], "correct": 2, "short": "The hose may have developed a set, or have been manufactured with a pre-set shape, and must be supported to maintain its shape.", "expl": "Teflon hose 'remembers' its shape. After time in service it may take a permanent set (or it was manufactured with a preformed bend), so when you temporarily remove it you must support it and not straighten it — forcing it flat can crack the liner. Store and reinstall it in its set shape.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-18", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8210", "q": "A scratch or nick in aluminum tubing can be repaired provided it does not", "options": ["appear in the heel of a bend.", "appear on the inside of a bend.", "exceed 10 percent of the tube OD on a straight section."], "correct": 0, "short": "appear in the heel of a bend.", "expl": "A scratch or nick may be repaired only if it is NOT in the heel (outside radius) of a bend. The heel is already stretched and highly stressed, so damage there can't be safely dressed out — the tube must be replaced.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-14", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8211", "q": "Which of the following hose materials are compatible with phosphate-ester base hydraulic fluids?", "options": ["Neoprene and Teflon.", "Butyl and Teflon.", "Teflon and Buna-N."], "correct": 1, "short": "Butyl and Teflon.", "expl": "Phosphate-ester based hydraulic fluid (Skydrol) attacks ordinary rubber. The hose materials that stand up to it are Butyl and Teflon. Buna-N (nitrile) and neoprene are for petroleum-base fluids and must NOT be used with Skydrol.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-16", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8212", "q": "Which tubings have the characteristics (high strength, abrasion resistance) necessary for use in a high-pressure (3,000 PSI) hydraulic system for operation of landing gear and flaps?", "options": ["2024-T or 5052-0 aluminum alloy.", "1100-1/2H or 3003-1/2H aluminum alloy.", "Corrosion-resistant steel annealed or 1/4H."], "correct": 2, "short": "Corrosion-resistant steel annealed or 1/4H.", "expl": "A 3,000 psi landing-gear/flap hydraulic system needs the high strength and abrasion resistance of corrosion-resistant steel tubing (annealed CRES 304 or 1/4-hard). Soft aluminum alloys like 2024-T, 5052-0, 1100, or 3003 are for low- and medium-pressure lines, not 3,000 psi service.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-1", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8213", "q": "When installing bonded clamps to support metal tubing,", "options": ["remove paint or anodizing from tube at clamp location.", "paint removal from tube is not recommended as it will inhibit corrosion.", "paint clamp and tube after clamp installation to prevent corrosion."], "correct": 0, "short": "remove paint or anodizing from tube at clamp location.", "expl": "Bonded clamps secure metal fluid lines and must make metal-to-metal contact to carry electrical bonding. So you remove the paint or anodizing from the tube where the clamp grips — painting over it would defeat the bond, not help corrosion.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-22", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8214", "q": "In a metal tubing installation,", "options": ["rigid straight line runs are preferable.", "tension is undesirable because pressurization will cause it to expand and shift.", "a tube may be pulled in line if the nut will start on the threaded coupling."], "correct": 1, "short": "tension is undesirable because pressurization will cause it to expand and shift.", "expl": "In a metal-tubing installation you avoid built-in tension: a line under tension will try to expand and shift when the system is pressurized, straining the fittings. Rigid dead-straight runs are NOT preferred (you want bends), and a tube must never be sprung or pulled into alignment just because the nut will start.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-22", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8214-1", "q": "Which of the following tools is best to use when cutting soft aluminum tubing?", "options": ["Angle grinder with cut-off wheel.", "Circular-saw equipped with an abrasive cutting wheel.", "Hand operated wheel-type tubing cutter."], "correct": 2, "short": "Hand operated wheel-type tubing cutter.", "expl": "Cut soft aluminum tubing with a hand-operated wheel-type tubing cutter — it makes a clean, square cut with minimal distortion. Abrasive cut-off wheels and circular saws generate heat and metal chips and leave a ragged end unsuited to flaring.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-2", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8214-2", "q": "The primary purpose of providing suitable bends in fluid and pneumatic metal tubing runs is to", "options": ["prevent excessive stress on the tubing.", "clear obstacles and make turns in aircraft structures.", "provide for access within aircraft structures."], "correct": 0, "short": "prevent excessive stress on the tubing.", "expl": "The main reason for putting bends in a fluid or pneumatic line is to prevent excessive stress: the bends let the tubing flex with vibration and with thermal expansion/contraction. Clearing obstacles is a bonus, but stress relief is the primary purpose.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-3", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8214-3", "q": "Which of the following statements is true regarding minimum allowable bend radii for 1.5 inches OD or less aluminum alloy and steel tubing of the same size?", "options": ["The minimum radius for steel is less than for aluminum.", "The minimum radius for steel is greater than for aluminum.", "The minimum radius is the same for both steel and aluminum."], "correct": 1, "short": "The minimum radius for steel is greater than for aluminum.", "expl": "For a given tube size (1.5-inch OD or less), steel needs a larger minimum bend radius than aluminum. Steel is stiffer and less forgiving, so it must be bent more gently — the minimum radius for steel is greater than for aluminum of the same size.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-2", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8215", "q": "A gas or fluid line marked with the letters PHDAN is", "options": ["a dual-purpose pneumatic and/or hydraulic line for normal and emergency system use.", "a pneumatic or hydraulic system drain or discharge line.", "used to carry a hazardous substance."], "correct": 2, "short": "used to carry a hazardous substance.", "expl": "A line marked PHDAN is carrying a Physically Hazardous or Dangerous substance — a warning that the contents (such as oxygen, nitrogen, or a toxic/flammable fluid) are hazardous. It is not a 'drain,' 'discharge,' or 'dual-purpose' code.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-7", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8215-1", "q": "Which statement is true regarding the variety of symbols utilized on the identifying color-code bands that are currently used on aircraft plumbing lines?", "options": ["Symbols are always black against a white background regardless of line content.", "Symbols are composed of various single colors according to line content.", "Symbols are composed of one to three contrasting colors according to line content."], "correct": 0, "short": "Symbols are always black against a white background regardless of line content.", "expl": "On aircraft plumbing identification bands the geometric symbols are printed in black against a white background regardless of what the line carries — the color coding is carried by the tape/band colors and words, while the symbol itself stays black-on-white so it reads the same everywhere.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-7", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8215-2", "q": "If a flared tube coupling nut is overtightened, where is the tube most likely to be weakened/damaged?", "options": ["Along the entire length of the sleeve and tube interface.", "At the sleeve, fitting nut, or tube flare.", "At the edge of the sleeve and straight portion of the tube."], "correct": 1, "short": "At the sleeve, fitting nut, or tube flare.", "expl": "Over-torquing a flared-tube coupling nut concentrates the damage right at the working joint — the sleeve, the fitting nut, and the tube flare. That's where the metal is thinnest and most worked, so that's where an over-tight nut cracks or wipes the flare.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-11", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8217", "q": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": ["Unbonded clamps are used for support when installing metal tubing.", "Bonded clamps are used when installing wiring.", "Unbonded clamps are used for support when installing wiring."], "correct": 2, "short": "Unbonded clamps are used for support when installing wiring.", "expl": "The pairing is: bonded clamps for metal tubing (they need the electrical bond), and unbonded (cushioned) clamps only for securing wiring. So the true statement is that unbonded clamps are used for wiring.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-22", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8217-1", "q": "Which statement is true regarding flattening of tubing in bends?", "options": ["The small diameter of the flattened portion must not be less than 75 percent of the original outside diameter.", "The small diameter of the flattened portion must not be less than 65 percent of the original outside diameter.", "The small diameter portion in the bend cannot exceed more than 75 percent of the diameter of straight tubing."], "correct": 0, "short": "The small diameter of the flattened portion must not be less than 75 percent of the original outside diameter.", "expl": "When you bend tubing, some flattening is unavoidable, but the small (flattened) diameter must not be less than 75 percent of the original outside diameter. Flatten it more than that and flow is choked and the tube is weakened — reject the bend.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-3", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}, {"id": "8218", "q": "A 3/8 inch aircraft high pressure flexible hose as compared to 3/8 inch metal tubing used in the same system will", "options": ["have higher flow capabilities.", "have equivalent flow characteristics.", "usually have interchangeable applications."], "correct": 1, "short": "have equivalent flow characteristics.", "expl": "A 3/8-inch high-pressure flexible hose and 3/8-inch metal tubing in the same system have equivalent flow characteristics — the hose's bore is built to match the tube it replaces. It does not magically flow more, and hose and rigid tube are not freely interchangeable in application.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-16", "topicName": "Fluid Lines, Fittings & Hoses", "fig": null}]}, {"code": "GNDOPS", "title": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "ref": null, "qs": [{"id": "8191-3", "q": "Which should be accomplished before jacking an aircraft?", "options": ["Install critical stress panels or plates.", "Determine that the fuel tanks are empty.", "Make sure the aircraft is leveled laterally."], "correct": 0, "short": "Install critical stress panels or plates.", "expl": "Some aircraft can't take the point loads of jacking without help. Before you jack them, install the required critical stress panels or plates so the jack pads spread the load into the structure. Leveling and fuel state matter for weighing, but the stress panels are the go/no-go item before the aircraft leaves the ground.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 6-14", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8291-1", "q": "Why is it generally necessary to jack an aircraft indoors for weighing?", "options": ["So aircraft may be placed in a level position.", "So that air currents do not destabilize the scales.", "So weighing scales may be calibrated to 0 pounds."], "correct": 1, "short": "So that air currents do not destabilize the scales.", "expl": "Weighing is done indoors so that air currents (wind, drafts, prop wash) can't push on the airframe and make the scales read high or low or bounce around. Steady, still air is what gives a repeatable, accurate weight — leveling and zeroing are separate steps.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 6-5", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8308", "q": "During starting of a turbine powerplant using compressed air starter, a hung start occurred. Select the proper procedure.", "options": ["Shut off the fuel.", "Re-engage the starter.", "Shut the engine down."], "correct": 2, "short": "Shut the engine down.", "expl": "A hung start means the engine lit off but hangs at a low RPM below idle and won't accelerate. The correct action is to shut the engine down — leaving it stuck there over-temps it. You do not keep motoring or re-engage the starter into a running engine.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-17", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8309", "q": "A hung start in a jet engine is often caused by", "options": ["the starter cutting off too soon.", "malfunctions in the ignition system.", "an excessively rich air-fuel mixture."], "correct": 0, "short": "the starter cutting off too soon.", "expl": "A hung start is usually the starter quitting too soon: the starter cuts off before the engine reaches self-sustaining speed, so the engine stalls out at a sub-idle RPM and just sits there. Enough starter assist for long enough is the cure.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-17", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8310", "q": "Which statement below reflects a typical requirement when towing some aircraft?", "options": ["Discharge all hydraulic pressure to prevent accidental operation of the nosewheel steering mechanism.", "If the aircraft has a steerable nosewheel, the torque-link lock should be set to full swivel.", "Tailwheel aircraft must be towed backwards."], "correct": 1, "short": "If the aircraft has a steerable nosewheel, the torque-link lock should be set to full swivel.", "expl": "On an aircraft with a steerable nosewheel, you set the torque-link (scissors) lock to full swivel before towing. That disconnects the steering so the nose gear can caster freely and the tow bar isn't fighting the steering mechanism.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-19", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8311", "q": "Which statement is true regarding tiedown of small aircraft?", "options": ["The aircraft should be headed downwind in order to eliminate or minimize wing lift.", "Leave the nosewheel or tailwheel unlocked.", "Nylon or dacron rope is preferred to manila rope."], "correct": 2, "short": "Nylon or dacron rope is preferred to manila rope.", "expl": "For tiedown, synthetic rope (nylon or dacron) is preferred over manila: manila shrinks when wet and can rot, while synthetic line holds its strength. Head the aircraft into the wind and lock the nose/tail wheel — not downwind, not unlocked.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-8", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8312", "q": "When approaching the front of an idling jet engine, the hazard area extends forward of the engine approximately", "options": ["25 feet.", "10 feet.", "15 feet."], "correct": 0, "short": "25 feet.", "expl": "The suction at the inlet of an idling jet engine is strong enough to pull a person or debris in from well ahead of it — treat roughly 25 feet in front as the hazard zone at idle (much more at higher power). Stay outside it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-4", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8314", "q": "(Refer to Figure 50.) Identify the signal to engage rotor on a rotorcraft.", "options": ["1.", "3.", "2."], "correct": 1, "short": "3.", "expl": "On the standard hand-signal chart, 'engage rotor' is signal 3 in the figure — the marshaller's motion telling the pilot to bring the rotor into engagement. Match the drawn arm motion to the chart rather than guessing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-23", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": "50"}, {"id": "8315", "q": "If a radial engine has been shut down for more than 30 minutes, the propeller should be rotated through at least two revolutions to", "options": ["check for leaks.", "prime the engine.", "check for hydraulic lock."], "correct": 2, "short": "check for hydraulic lock.", "expl": "A radial (or any big inverted/horizontally opposed) engine that has sat more than about 30 minutes can pool oil in the lower cylinders. Pull the prop through at least two revolutions by hand first to check for a hydraulic lock — trapped oil that would bend a rod if you cranked it on the starter.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-12", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8316", "q": "The priming of a fuel injected horizontally opposed engine is accomplished by placing the fuel control lever in the", "options": ["FULL-RICH position.", "IDLE-CUTOFF position.", "AUTO-RICH position."], "correct": 0, "short": "FULL-RICH position.", "expl": "A fuel-injected horizontally opposed engine is primed by moving the fuel control (mixture) lever to FULL-RICH and running the boost pump, which pushes fuel to the injector nozzles. IDLE-CUTOFF shuts fuel off, and AUTO-RICH is a pressure-carburetor term, not this system.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-13", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8317", "q": "The most important condition to be monitored during start after fuel flow begins in a turbine engine is the", "options": ["RPM.", "EGT, TIT, or ITT.", "oil pressure."], "correct": 1, "short": "EGT, TIT, or ITT.", "expl": "The instant fuel flow begins in a turbine start, the number-one thing to watch is the turbine gas temperature — EGT, TIT, or ITT depending on the engine. A rapid over-temp (hot start) is what destroys hot-section parts, so your eyes go to that gauge, ready to cut fuel.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-17", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8317-1", "q": "Which of the following conditions has the most potential for causing engine damage when starting or attempting to start a turbine engine?", "options": ["Hung start.", "Cold start.", "Hot start."], "correct": 2, "short": "Hot start.", "expl": "Of the abnormal starts, the hot start has the most potential to wreck an engine because it burns the hot-section components with excessive temperature. A hung start is bad but is a no-acceleration condition; a hot start is an over-temperature condition — heat is what does the damage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-17", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8318", "q": "How is a flooded engine, equipped with a float-type carburetor, cleared of excessive fuel?", "options": ["Crank the engine with the starter or by hand, with the mixture control in cutoff, ignition switch off, and the throttle fully open, until the fuel charge has been cleared.", "Turn off the fuel and the ignition. Discontinue the starting attempt until the excess fuel has cleared.", "Crank the engine with the starter or by hand, with the mixture control in cutoff, ignition switch on, and the throttle fully open, until the excess fuel has cleared or until the engine starts."], "correct": 0, "short": "Crank the engine with the starter or by hand, with the mixture control in cutoff, ignition switch off, and the throttle fully open, until the fuel charge has been cleared.", "expl": "Clear a flooded float-carburetor engine by motoring it with the mixture in idle-cutoff, the ignition OFF, and the throttle fully open, so airflow scavenges the raw fuel out. Ignition stays OFF during clearing so a backfire can't ignite the excess fuel.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-13", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8319", "q": "(Refer to Figure 51.) Which marshalling signal should be given if a taxiing aircraft is in imminent danger of striking an object?", "options": ["1 or 3.", "3.", "2."], "correct": 1, "short": "3.", "expl": "If a taxiing aircraft is about to strike something, the marshaller gives the emergency-stop signal (arms crossed/waved overhead) — signal 3 in the figure — which overrides the normal 'stop.' It's the one signal meant to halt movement instantly.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-22", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": "51"}, {"id": "8320", "q": "Generally, when an induction fire occurs during starting of a reciprocating engine, the first course of action should be to", "options": ["discharge carbon dioxide from a fire extinguisher into the air intake of the engine.", "close the throttle.", "continue cranking and start the engine if possible."], "correct": 2, "short": "continue cranking and start the engine if possible.", "expl": "When an induction (intake) fire flares during a reciprocating-engine start, the first move is to keep cranking and try to start the engine — a running engine sucks the fire back through the induction system and snuffs it. Stopping the crank leaves the fire fed.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-13", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8320-1", "q": "If a fire develops in an engine during the starting procedure, the aviation mechanic should", "options": ["continue cranking to start the engine and extinguish the fire.", "discontinue the start attempt and allow the fireguard to extinguish the fire.", "continue cranking and allow the fireguard to extinguish the fire."], "correct": 0, "short": "continue cranking to start the engine and extinguish the fire.", "expl": "Same principle for any engine fire during start: continue cranking to get the engine running, which draws the fire into the engine and extinguishes it. You don't quit and hand it straight to the fireguard while flames are being fed by fuel in the induction system.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-13", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8321", "q": "When starting and ground operating an aircraft’s engine, the aircraft should be positioned to head into the wind primarily", "options": ["to aid in achieving and maintaining the proper air flow into the engine induction system.", "for engine cooling purposes.", "to help cancel out engine torque effect."], "correct": 1, "short": "for engine cooling purposes.", "expl": "You point the aircraft into the wind for ground running primarily for engine cooling — the headwind forces air through the cooling fins and oil cooler while the aircraft sits still. Induction ram and torque cancellation are minor here; keeping the engine cool is the reason.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-13", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8322", "q": "When approaching the rear of an idling turbojet or turbofan engine, the hazard area extends aft of the engine approximately", "options": ["200 feet.", "50 feet.", "100 feet."], "correct": 2, "short": "100 feet.", "expl": "The exhaust blast behind an idling turbojet/turbofan is a hazard for roughly 100 feet aft at idle — hot, high-velocity gas that can knock a person down or throw debris. Stay clear of that cone; the distance grows sharply with power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-4", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8323", "q": "If a hot start occurs during starting of a turbine powerplant, what is the likely cause?", "options": ["The air-fuel mixture was excessively rich.", "The starting unit overheated.", "The ambient air temperature was too high (over 100°F)."], "correct": 0, "short": "The air-fuel mixture was excessively rich.", "expl": "A hot start (turbine temperature spiking too high during light-off) is caused by an excessively rich fuel-air mixture — too much fuel for the airflow the starter is providing, so combustion runs hot. It's a fuel-scheduling/airflow problem, not the starter overheating.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-17", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8326", "q": "When towing a large aircraft", "options": ["a person should be in the flight deck to watch for obstructions.", "a person should be in the flight deck to operate the brakes.", "persons should be stationed at the nose, each wingtip, and the empennage at all times."], "correct": 1, "short": "a person should be in the flight deck to operate the brakes.", "expl": "When towing a large aircraft, a qualified person must be in the flight deck to operate the brakes in case the aircraft has to be stopped independently of the tug. Watching for obstructions is the job of the wing/tail walkers, not the brake rider.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-17", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8327", "q": "Weathervaning tendency is greatest when taxiing", "options": ["both nosewheel and tailwheel-type airplanes in a quartering tailwind.", "a nosewheel-type airplane in a quartering headwind.", "a tailwheel-type airplane in a direct crosswind."], "correct": 2, "short": "a tailwheel-type airplane in a direct crosswind.", "expl": "Weathervaning — the tendency to swing the nose into the wind — is worst for a tailwheel airplane in a direct crosswind. The large side area behind the main gear and the light, free-swiveling tailwheel let the wind pivot the tail downwind, so the nose weathervanes into it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-3, Ground Operations (Taxiing in wind)", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8328", "q": "When taxiing an airplane with a quartering tailwind, the elevators and", "options": ["upwind aileron should be held in the down position.", "upwind aileron should be held in the up position.", "both ailerons should be kept in the neutral position."], "correct": 0, "short": "upwind aileron should be held in the down position.", "expl": "Taxiing with a quartering tailwind, you 'dive away from the wind': hold the elevators down and the upwind aileron down. That keeps the tailwind from getting under the upwind wing and the tail, which is exactly what could flip a light aircraft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-3, Ground Operations (Taxiing in wind)", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8329", "q": "When taxiing (or towing) an aircraft, a flashing red light from the control tower means", "options": ["stop and wait for a green light.", "move clear of the runway/taxiway immediately.", "return to starting point."], "correct": 1, "short": "move clear of the runway/taxiway immediately.", "expl": "A flashing red light gun signal from the tower to an aircraft on the ground means taxi clear of the runway in use — get off the runway/taxiway immediately. (Steady red means stop; flashing red means clear the area.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-21", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8330", "q": "A person should approach or leave a helicopter the pilot’s field of vision whenever the engine is running in order to avoid", "options": ["the main rotor.", "blowing dust or debris caused by rotor downwash.", "the tail rotor."], "correct": 2, "short": "the tail rotor.", "expl": "Always approach and leave a running helicopter within the pilot's field of vision, and do it to avoid the tail rotor, which spins invisibly and at head height and has killed people who walked aft. Stay forward where the pilot can see you and well clear of the tail.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-20", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8331", "q": "When taxiing (or towing) an aircraft, a flashing white light from the control tower means", "options": ["return to starting point.", "move clear of the runway/taxiway immediately.", "OK to proceed but use extreme caution."], "correct": 0, "short": "return to starting point.", "expl": "A flashing white light gun signal to an aircraft on the ground means return to your starting point on the airport. It's the tower's 'go back where you came from' instruction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-21", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8332", "q": "When taxiing (or towing) an aircraft, an alternating red and green light from the control tower means", "options": ["move clear of the runway/taxiway immediately.", "OK to proceed but use extreme caution.", "return to starting point."], "correct": 1, "short": "OK to proceed but use extreme caution.", "expl": "An alternating red-and-green light gun signal is a general warning: exercise extreme caution. It doesn't grant or deny clearance by itself — it tells you to proceed very carefully and be ready for anything.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-21", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8333", "q": "When stopping a nosewheel-type airplane after taxiing, the nosewheel should be left", "options": ["unlocked and pointed straight ahead.", "turned at a small angle towards uphill if the parking area is not perfectly flat.", "pointed straight ahead."], "correct": 2, "short": "pointed straight ahead.", "expl": "After taxiing a nosewheel airplane to a stop, leave the nosewheel pointed straight ahead. A cocked nosewheel puts side loads on the strut and makes the next movement lurch; straight-ahead is the neutral, low-stress parking position.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-18", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}, {"id": "8334", "q": "When first starting to move an aircraft while taxiing, it is important to", "options": ["test the brakes.", "closely monitor the instruments.", "notify the control tower."], "correct": 0, "short": "test the brakes.", "expl": "The first thing to do when the aircraft just begins to roll while taxiing is test the brakes — confirm you can actually stop before you build any speed. Everything else waits until you know the brakes work.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-18", "topicName": "Ground Operations — Start, Taxi, Towing & Servicing", "fig": null}]}, {"code": "FUEL", "title": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "ref": null, "qs": [{"id": "8313", "q": "Which of the following is the most satisfactory extinguishing agent for use on a carburetor or intake fire?", "options": ["Carbon dioxide.", "Dry chemical.", "A fine, water mist."], "correct": 0, "short": "Carbon dioxide.", "expl": "The best extinguishing agent for a carburetor or intake fire is carbon dioxide (CO2). It smothers the fire without leaving residue in the induction system, and unlike a water mist it won't cause corrosion or hydraulic problems inside the engine.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-5", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8324", "q": "What effect, if any, will aviation gasoline mixed with jet fuel have on a turbine engine?", "options": ["No appreciable effect.", "The tetraethyl lead in the gasoline forms deposits on the turbine blades.", "The tetraethyl lead in the gasoline forms deposits on the compressor blades."], "correct": 1, "short": "The tetraethyl lead in the gasoline forms deposits on the turbine blades.", "expl": "Put aviation gasoline into a turbine engine and the tetraethyl lead in the avgas cooks into hard deposits on the turbine blades (the hot section downstream of combustion). It's the lead — not the fuel energy — that does the harm, and it lands on the turbine, not the compressor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-25", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8325", "q": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": ["Viscosity has no relation to contamination of fuel.", "Jet fuel is of lower viscosity than aviation gasoline and therefore holds contaminants more readily.", "Jet fuel is of higher viscosity than aviation gasoline and therefore holds contaminants more readily."], "correct": 2, "short": "Jet fuel is of higher viscosity than aviation gasoline and therefore holds contaminants more readily.", "expl": "Jet fuel (kerosene) is more viscous — thicker and oilier — than aviation gasoline. That higher viscosity lets it hold onto contaminants like water and solids more readily, so jet fuel needs especially careful filtering and sumping.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-32B, Ch. 2 (Engine Fuel & Fuel Metering Systems)", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8335", "q": "The color of 100LL fuel is", "options": ["blue.", "colorless or straw.", "red."], "correct": 0, "short": "blue.", "expl": "100LL avgas is dyed blue. The color code lets you confirm at the truck and the fill port that you're putting the right grade in: 80 is red, 100 is green, 100LL is blue, 115 is purple. Jet fuel is handled as black on equipment though the fuel itself is clear to straw.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-26", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8336", "q": "How are aviation fuels, which possess greater antiknock qualities than 100 octane, classified?", "options": ["According to the milliliters of lead.", "By performance numbers.", "By reference to normal heptane."], "correct": 1, "short": "By performance numbers.", "expl": "Below 100 octane, fuels are rated by octane number; above 100 there's no more octane scale, so fuels with greater antiknock value than 100-octane are graded by performance numbers instead. That's why you see '100/130' — 100 octane lean, 130 performance number rich.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-32B, Ch. 1 (Aircraft Engines — fuel ratings)", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8338", "q": "Both gasoline and kerosene have certain advantages for use as turbine fuel. Which statement is true in reference to the advantages of each?", "options": ["Kerosene has a higher heat energy/value per unit weight than gasoline.", "Gasoline has a higher heat energy/value per unit volume than kerosene.", "Kerosene has a higher heat energy/value per unit volume than gasoline."], "correct": 2, "short": "Kerosene has a higher heat energy/value per unit volume than gasoline.", "expl": "Kerosene (jet fuel) packs more heat energy into each gallon than gasoline — it has a higher heat value per unit VOLUME. Gasoline wins slightly per unit weight, but because a turbine burns huge volumes, the higher energy-per-volume of kerosene is the meaningful advantage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-32B, Ch. 2 (Engine Fuel & Fuel Metering Systems)", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8339", "q": "What must accompany fuel vaporization?", "options": ["An absorption of heat.", "A decrease in vapor pressure.", "A reduction in volume."], "correct": 0, "short": "An absorption of heat.", "expl": "Turning a liquid into vapor always takes heat in — vaporization is accompanied by absorption of heat (latent heat). That heat has to come from somewhere, which is why vaporizing fuel cools the induction charge and why highly volatile fuel can chill lines enough to cause trouble.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-32B, Ch. 2 (Engine Fuel & Fuel Metering Systems)", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8340", "q": "Characteristics of detonation are", "options": ["cylinder pressure remains the same, excessive cylinder head temperature, and a decrease in engine power.", "rapid rise in cylinder pressure, excessive cylinder head temperature, and a decrease in engine power.", "rapid rise in cylinder pressure, cylinder head temperature normal, and a decrease in engine power."], "correct": 1, "short": "rapid rise in cylinder pressure, excessive cylinder head temperature, and a decrease in engine power.", "expl": "Detonation is the fuel-air charge exploding instead of burning smoothly. Its signature is a rapid, spiking rise in cylinder pressure, excessive cylinder-head temperature, and a loss of engine power — the sharp pressure spike and heat are what hammer pistons and valves.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-32B, Ch. 1 (Aircraft Engines — detonation)", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8341", "q": "A fuel that vaporizes too readily may cause", "options": ["hard starting.", "detonation.", "vapor lock."], "correct": 2, "short": "vapor lock.", "expl": "A fuel that vaporizes too readily (too volatile) can flash to vapor inside the lines, pumps, or fittings before it reaches the metering device. Those vapor pockets block the flow — that's vapor lock, a partial or complete interruption of fuel to the engine.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-32B, Ch. 2 (Engine Fuel & Fuel Metering Systems)", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8342", "q": "Jet fuel number identifiers are", "options": ["type numbers and have no relation to the fuel’s performance in the aircraft engine.", "performance numbers to designate the volatility of the fuel.", "performance numbers and are relative to the fuel’s performance in the aircraft engine."], "correct": 0, "short": "type numbers and have no relation to the fuel’s performance in the aircraft engine.", "expl": "Jet fuel numbers (JET A, JET A-1, JET B) are type designations — they identify the kind of fuel, not a performance rating. Unlike avgas octane/performance numbers, a jet-fuel number tells you nothing about how it 'performs' in the engine.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-32B, Ch. 2 (Engine Fuel & Fuel Metering Systems)", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}, {"id": "8343", "q": "The main differences between grades 100 and 100LL fuel are", "options": ["volatility and lead content.", "lead content and color.", "volatility, lead content, and color."], "correct": 1, "short": "lead content and color.", "expl": "Grades 100 and 100LL have the same antiknock rating; the real differences are lead content (100LL = 'low lead,' with less tetraethyl lead than grade 100) and color (100 is green, 100LL is blue). Volatility is essentially the same, so it's lead and color.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 1-25", "topicName": "Aviation Fuels & Fire Safety", "fig": null}]}, {"code": "RECORDS", "title": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "ref": null, "qs": [{"id": "8443", "q": "Where is the record of compliance with Airworthiness Directives or manufacturers’ service bulletins normally indicated?", "options": ["Aircraft maintenance records.", "FAA Form 337.", "Flight manual."], "correct": 0, "short": "Aircraft maintenance records.", "expl": "Compliance with Airworthiness Directives and manufacturers' service bulletins is written into the aircraft maintenance records — the permanent logbook history. It doesn't live in the flight manual, and Form 337 is only for major repairs/alterations, not routine AD entries.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-8 (14 CFR 43.9 / 91.417)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8444", "q": "If work performed on an aircraft has been done satisfactorily, the signature of an authorized person on the maintenance records for maintenance or alterations performed constitutes", "options": ["approval of the aircraft for return to service.", "approval for return to service only for the work performed.", "verification that the maintenance or alterations were performed referencing approved maintenance data."], "correct": 1, "short": "approval for return to service only for the work performed.", "expl": "A signature on the maintenance records for a job is an approval for return to service of ONLY the specific work that was performed — not a blanket blessing of the whole aircraft. The person is certifying that this task was done correctly, nothing more.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 43.9)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8448", "q": "Where should the aviation mechanic find this entry? “Removed right wing from aircraft and removed skin from outer 6 feet. Repaired buckled spar 49 inches from tip accordance with figure 8 in the manufacturer’s structural repair manual No. 28-1.”", "options": ["Aircraft engine maintenance record.", "Aircraft minor repair and alteration record.", "FAA Form 337."], "correct": 2, "short": "FAA Form 337.", "expl": "Removing a wing, replacing large skin areas, and repairing a buckled spar is a major repair to a primary structure — that requires an FAA Form 337. Routine engine or minor-repair records wouldn't capture a structural major repair like this.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.9, App. B)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8450", "q": "Which aircraft record entry is the best description of the replacement of several damaged heli-coils in casting?", "options": ["Eight 1/4—20 inch standard heli-coils were replaced. The damaged inserts were extracted, the tapped holes gaged, then new inserts installed, and tangs removed.", "Eight 1/4—20 inch standard heli-coils were installed in place of damaged ones.", "Eight 1/4—20 inch standard heli-coil inserts were repaired by replacing the damaged inserts with a lock-type insert, after the tapped holes were checked for corrosion."], "correct": 0, "short": "Eight 1/4—20 inch standard heli-coils were replaced. The damaged inserts were extracted, the tapped holes gaged, then new inserts installed, and tangs removed.", "expl": "A good record entry states exactly what was done: the damaged inserts were extracted, the tapped holes gaged, new standard heli-coils installed, and the tangs removed. The vague 'installed in place of damaged ones' and the entry that calls them 'lock-type' or claims a 'repair' misdescribe the actual work.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 43.9)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8451", "q": "Which maintenance record entry best describes the action taken for a control cable showing approximately 20 percent wear on several of the individual outer wires at a fairlead?", "options": ["Removed and replaced the control cable and rerigged the system.", "Wear within acceptable limits, repair not necessary.", "Cable repositioned, worn area moved away from fairlead."], "correct": 1, "short": "Wear within acceptable limits, repair not necessary.", "expl": "About 20 percent wear on several outer wires of a control cable at a fairlead is within acceptable limits, so the honest entry is 'wear within acceptable limits, repair not necessary.' You don't record a replacement or a reposition that didn't happen — the record must match the work.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8452", "q": "Which maintenance record entry best describes the action taken for a .125-inch deep dent in a straight section of 1/2-inch aluminum alloy tubing?", "options": ["Dented section removed and replaced with identical new tubing flared to 45°.", "Dent within acceptable limits, repair not necessary.", "Dented section removed and replaced with identical new tubing flared to 37°."], "correct": 2, "short": "Dented section removed and replaced with identical new tubing flared to 37°.", "expl": "A 0.125-inch-deep dent in 1/2-inch aluminum tubing is well past dent limits, so the section is removed and replaced with identical new tubing, and aluminum for AN fittings is flared to 37 degrees. The 45-degree entry is wrong because AN aluminum tube flares are 37 degrees, not 45.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 9-3", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8452-1", "q": "Which statement is true regarding the requirements for maintenance record format?", "options": ["Any format that provides record continuity and includes the required information may be used.", "The format provided by the manufacturer of the aircraft must be retained.", "Any desired change from manufacturer provided format requires approval from the Federal Aviation Administration."], "correct": 0, "short": "Any format that provides record continuity and includes the required information may be used.", "expl": "There is no mandated form for maintenance records — any format is acceptable as long as it provides record continuity and contains all the required information. You are not locked into the manufacturer's format, and changing formats needs no FAA approval.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 91.417)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8452-3", "q": "For aircraft operated under Part 91, when is aircraft total time required to be recorded in aircraft maintenance records?", "options": ["After satisfactorily completing maintenance, preventive maintenance, rebuilding, and alteration (excluding inspections).", "After satisfactorily completing inspections.", "After satisfactorily completing airframe, component, or propeller maintenance."], "correct": 1, "short": "After satisfactorily completing inspections.", "expl": "Under Part 91, aircraft total time in service is the entry required when you complete an inspection. The inspection entry is the one that captures total time; a plain maintenance/repair entry describes the work but isn't the total-time trigger.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 91.417 / 43.11)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8452-4", "q": "For aircraft operated under Part 91, what difference is there, if any, between the record entry requirements for maintenance (e.g., repair or alteration) and the record entry requirements for inspections (beyond the description of the work performed and the type and extent of inspection)?", "options": ["There is no difference.", "Aircraft total time is required to be included only in the maintenance entry.", "Aircraft total time is required to be included only in the inspection entry."], "correct": 2, "short": "Aircraft total time is required to be included only in the inspection entry.", "expl": "Beyond describing the work, the difference is that aircraft total time in service must be included in the inspection entry (43.11) but is not required in the ordinary maintenance/repair entry (43.9). So the total-time requirement is unique to the inspection record.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 43.9 vs 43.11)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8452-5", "q": "If more space is needed for a work description entered on FAA Form 337, what information should be included on the attached sheet(s), in addition to the rest of the work description?", "options": ["Aircraft nationality and registration mark, and the date the work was accomplished.", "Make, model, and serial number of the aircraft.", "Name, date, and office designator of the FAA inspector from the supervising office."], "correct": 0, "short": "Aircraft nationality and registration mark, and the date the work was accomplished.", "expl": "If the work description overflows onto attached sheets, each attached sheet must carry the aircraft nationality and registration mark and the date the work was accomplished, so a loose page can always be tied back to the right aircraft and event.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.9, App. B)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8452-6", "q": "A person installing a product, part, or appliance on a type certificated product must make certain that the item’s records document the", "options": ["source from which the part came.", "product, part, or material meets FAA airworthiness standards.", "product or material was not produced under an FAA production approval."], "correct": 1, "short": "product, part, or material meets FAA airworthiness standards.", "expl": "When you install a product, part, or appliance on a type-certificated product, its records must document that the item meets FAA airworthiness standards (approved/eligible for installation). Tracing the mere 'source' isn't the point — proving airworthiness eligibility is.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-8 (14 CFR 43.9)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8453", "q": "Which aircraft record entry best describes a repair of a dent in a tubular steel structure dented at a cluster?", "options": ["Removed and replaced the damaged member.", "Filled the damaged area with a molten metal and dressed to the original contour.", "Welded a reinforcing plate over the dented area."], "correct": 2, "short": "Welded a reinforcing plate over the dented area.", "expl": "A dent at a cluster (a welded joint) in tubular steel structure is repaired by welding a reinforcing plate over the dented area — the standard cluster repair. You don't just 'remove and replace the member' as a description, and you never fill a dent with molten metal and dress it smooth.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8454-1", "q": "For aircraft operated under Part 91, which of the following records must be retained for at least one year, or until the work is repeated or superseded?", "options": ["Records of maintenance, alterations, preventive maintenance, 100-hour, annual, and progressive inspections.", "Records of time since overhaul of items requiring overhaul on a time specified basis.", "Records of the current inspection status of the aircraft, including time since last required inspection."], "correct": 0, "short": "Records of maintenance, alterations, preventive maintenance, 100-hour, annual, and progressive inspections.", "expl": "The records that only have to be kept for one year (or until the work is repeated or superseded) are the records of maintenance, alterations, preventive maintenance, and the required inspections. These are the shorter-retention records, distinct from the permanent status records.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 91.417)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8454-2", "q": "A certificated mechanic without an inspection authorization who signs the appropriate block on FAA Form 337 is doing what?", "options": ["Approving the work for return to service.", "Certifying that the work was done in accordance with the requirements of 14 CFR Part 43.", "Certifying the maintenance information used as FAA-approved data."], "correct": 1, "short": "Certifying that the work was done in accordance with the requirements of 14 CFR Part 43.", "expl": "A mechanic without an IA who signs the appropriate block on Form 337 is certifying that the work was done in accordance with 14 CFR Part 43. That signature covers the workmanship; it is NOT an approval for return to service and does NOT certify the data as FAA-approved.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.9 / 43.13)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8454-3", "q": "For aircraft operated under Part 91, which of the following records must be retained and transferred with the aircraft when it is sold?", "options": ["Records of maintenance, alterations, preventive maintenance, 100-hour, annual, and progressive inspections.", "Records of inspections performed in accordance with 14 CFR Part 43, Appendix D.", "Records of the current status of applicable ADs, and date and time when recurring ADs are next due."], "correct": 2, "short": "Records of the current status of applicable ADs, and date and time when recurring ADs are next due.", "expl": "The records that must be retained and transferred with the aircraft when it is sold are the permanent-status records: the current AD status, plus the method of compliance and when each recurring AD is next due. Those follow the aircraft forever, not just one year.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 91.417 / 91.419)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8455-1", "q": "When a discrepancy list is provided to an aircraft owner or operator after an inspection, it says in effect that", "options": ["except for these discrepancies, the item inspected is airworthy.", "the item inspected is unairworthy.", "the item inspected may or may not be airworthy depending on the discrepancies found."], "correct": 0, "short": "except for these discrepancies, the item inspected is airworthy.", "expl": "A discrepancy list handed to the owner after an inspection says, in effect: except for these listed items, the aircraft is airworthy. It's the inspector's way of approving everything that passed while itemizing exactly what still needs correction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-18", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8455-2", "q": "In order to reconstruct lost or destroyed aircraft maintenance records, what is it necessary to establish?", "options": ["Dates of all maintenance, preventive maintenance and alterations.", "Total time-in-service of the airframe.", "Dates and/or times of all 100-hour, annual, or progressive inspections."], "correct": 1, "short": "Total time-in-service of the airframe.", "expl": "To rebuild lost or destroyed maintenance records, the one thing that must be established is the total time in service of the airframe — that's the anchor for reconstructing the aircraft's history. AD status and inspection currency get rebuilt around that baseline.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 91.417)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8456", "q": "When approving for return to service after maintenance or alteration, the approving person must enter in the maintenance record of the aircraft", "options": ["the date the maintenance or alteration was begun, a description (or reference to acceptable data) of work performed, the name of the person performing the work (if someone else), signature, and certificate number.", "a description (or reference to acceptable data) of work performed, date of completion, the name of the person performing the work (if someone else), signature, and certificate number.", "a description (or reference to acceptable data) of work performed, date of completion, the name of the person performing the work (if someone else), signature, certificate number, and kind of certificate held."], "correct": 2, "short": "a description (or reference to acceptable data) of work performed, date of completion, the name of the person performing the work (if someone else), signature, certificate number, and kind of certificate held.", "expl": "A complete return-to-service entry needs: a description of (or reference to acceptable data for) the work, the date of completion, the name of the person doing the work if different from the signer, that person's signature, certificate number, AND the kind of certificate held. The date of COMPLETION matters, not the date begun.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 43.9)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8457-1", "q": "When work is performed on an aircraft that necessitates the use of FAA Form 337, who should prepare the form?", "options": ["The person who performs or supervises the work.", "The person who approves for return to service.", "Either the person who approves for return to service, or the aircraft owner or operator."], "correct": 0, "short": "The person who performs or supervises the work.", "expl": "The Form 337 is prepared by the person who actually performs or supervises the work — they know what was done. It isn't the owner's job, and it isn't handed off to whoever later approves it for return to service.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.9)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8457-3", "q": "Which statement is true regarding the use of FAA Form 337?", "options": ["FAA Form 337 is authorized for use with both U.S. and foreign registered aircraft.", "FAA Form 337 is not authorized for use with other than U.S. registered aircraft.", "FAA Form 337 is authorized for use with U.S. registered aircraft, and foreign registered aircraft when located in the United States."], "correct": 1, "short": "FAA Form 337 is not authorized for use with other than U.S. registered aircraft.", "expl": "FAA Form 337 is a U.S. document: it is not authorized for use on other-than-U.S.-registered aircraft. It's tied to the FAA registry, so a foreign-registered aircraft doesn't get a 337.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8457-4", "q": "When performing a major repair or alteration, for which aircraft must an aviation mechanic submit Form 337 to the FAA?", "options": ["Form 337 is required for both U.S. and foreign-registered aircraft.", "Form 337 is required for all aircraft operated in U.S. airspace.", "Form 337 is required for U.S.-registered aircraft and may be requested for foreign aircraft."], "correct": 2, "short": "Form 337 is required for U.S.-registered aircraft and may be requested for foreign aircraft.", "expl": "For a major repair or alteration, Form 337 is required for U.S.-registered aircraft, and it may be requested (but isn't automatically required) for foreign aircraft. So it's mandatory only on U.S.-registered aircraft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.9)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8458", "q": "After making a certain repair to an aircraft engine that is to be returned to service, an FAA Form 337 is prepared. How many copies are required and what is the disposition of the completed forms?", "options": ["Two; one copy for the aircraft owner and one copy for the FAA.", "Two; one copy for the FAA and one copy for the permanent records of the repairing agency or individual.", "Three; one copy for the aircraft owner, one copy for the FAA, and one copy for the permanent records of the repairing agency or individual."], "correct": 0, "short": "Two; one copy for the aircraft owner and one copy for the FAA.", "expl": "For a major repair, two copies of Form 337 are executed: one goes to the aircraft owner and one to the FAA. (The repairing agency keeps a copy of its records, but the two formal 337 copies are owner and FAA.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.9)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8459", "q": "Who is responsible for upkeep of the required maintenance records for an aircraft?", "options": ["The maintaining repair station or authorized inspector.", "The aircraft owner.", "The maintaining certificated mechanic."], "correct": 1, "short": "The aircraft owner.", "expl": "Upkeep of the required maintenance records is the aircraft OWNER's (or operator's) responsibility. The mechanic or repair station makes the entries, but keeping the records complete and available belongs to the owner.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 91.417)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8462", "q": "An FAA Form 337 is used to record and document", "options": ["preventive and unscheduled maintenance, and special inspections.", "major and minor repairs, and major and minor alterations.", "major repairs and major alterations."], "correct": 2, "short": "major repairs and major alterations.", "expl": "FAA Form 337 is used to record and document major repairs and major alterations. Minor work and routine maintenance are captured by ordinary logbook entries, not by a 337.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.9)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8508", "q": "What is the maintenance recording responsibility of the person who complies with an Airworthiness Directive?", "options": ["Make an entry in the maintenance record of that equipment.", "Advise the aircraft owner/operator of the work performed.", "Advise the FAA office of the work performed, by submitting an FAA Form 337."], "correct": 0, "short": "Make an entry in the maintenance record of that equipment.", "expl": "The person who complies with an Airworthiness Directive records it by making an entry in the maintenance record of that equipment. AD compliance is a logbook entry — it does not by itself require a Form 337 or a separate letter to the FAA.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-8 (14 CFR 43.9 / 91.417)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}, {"id": "8516-2", "q": "How long are AD compliance records required to be kept?", "options": ["Until the work is repeated or superseded by other work.", "They shall be retained, and then transferred with the aircraft when it is sold.", "For one year after the work is performed, or until the work is repeated or superseded by other work."], "correct": 1, "short": "They shall be retained, and then transferred with the aircraft when it is sold.", "expl": "AD compliance records are permanent-status records: they must be retained and then transferred with the aircraft when it is sold. Unlike ordinary maintenance entries (one-year retention), AD status follows the aircraft for its life.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-13 (14 CFR 91.417)", "topicName": "Maintenance Records & FAA Form 337", "fig": null}]}, {"code": "ADTC", "title": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "ref": null, "qs": [{"id": "8446", "q": "What is the means by which the FAA notifies aircraft owners and other interested persons of unsafe conditions and prescribes the condition under which the product may continue to be operated?", "options": ["Airworthiness Directives.", "Aviation Maintenance Alerts.", "Aviation Safety Data."], "correct": 0, "short": "Airworthiness Directives.", "expl": "The Airworthiness Directive is how the FAA notifies owners of an unsafe condition and legally prescribes what has to be done for the product to keep flying. ADs are regulatory — they carry the force of law, unlike advisory alerts or safety data.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-8 (14 CFR Part 39)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8457-2", "q": "What is the status of data used as a basis for approving major repairs or alterations for return to service?", "options": ["Data must be least FAA-acceptable when it is used for that purpose.", "Data must be FAA-approved prior to its use for that purpose.", "Data may be FAA-approved after its use for that purpose."], "correct": 1, "short": "Data must be FAA-approved prior to its use for that purpose.", "expl": "Data used as the basis for approving a MAJOR repair or alteration for return to service must be FAA-approved BEFORE it is used for that purpose. You can't do the work on merely acceptable data and get it approved afterward — the approved data comes first.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.13)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8464-1", "q": "What is ATA iSpec 2200?", "options": ["Fuel specifications for transport-category airplanes.", "Airline transport catalog for aircraft parts.", "Standardized electronic documentation for aircraft models."], "correct": 2, "short": "Standardized electronic documentation for aircraft models.", "expl": "ATA iSpec 2200 is the Air Transport Association's standard for electronic, standardized technical documentation on large aircraft — it assigns the familiar ATA chapter numbers so maintenance data is arranged the same way regardless of the airplane. It's a documentation standard, not a fuel spec or parts catalog.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8492", "q": "Airworthiness Directives are issued primarily to", "options": ["correct an unsafe condition.", "provide information about malfunction or defect trends.", "present recommended maintenance procedures for correcting potentially hazardous defects."], "correct": 0, "short": "correct an unsafe condition.", "expl": "Airworthiness Directives exist for one primary purpose: to correct an unsafe condition on a product. They're not published to report defect trends or to 'recommend' procedures — an AD is a mandatory fix for a known unsafe condition.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-26 (14 CFR Part 39)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8493", "q": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": ["A Supplemental Type Certificate (STC) may only be issued to one applicant for the same design change.", "A Supplemental Type Certificate (STC) may be issued to more than one applicant for the same design change, providing each applicant shows compliance with the applicable airworthiness requirement.", "An installation of an item manufactured in accordance with the Technical Standard Order (TSO) system requires no further approval for installation in a particular aircraft."], "correct": 1, "short": "A Supplemental Type Certificate (STC) may be issued to more than one applicant for the same design change, providing each applicant shows compliance with the applicable airworthiness requirement.", "expl": "A Supplemental Type Certificate can be issued to more than one applicant for the same design change, as long as each applicant independently shows compliance with the applicable airworthiness requirements. The STC isn't a one-owner monopoly on a modification.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-27 (14 CFR Part 21)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8493-1", "q": "What does the acronym TSO mean regarding a type certificated aircraft part, material, component and/ or process?", "options": ["Training Specific Organization.", "Type Supplement Original.", "Technical Standard Order."], "correct": 2, "short": "Technical Standard Order.", "expl": "TSO stands for Technical Standard Order — a minimum performance standard the FAA issues for specified materials, parts, and appliances. A TSO authorization says the article meets that standard.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-4 (14 CFR Part 21)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8494", "q": "Primary responsibility for compliance with Airworthiness Directives lies with the", "options": ["aircraft owner or operator.", "certificated mechanic holding an Inspection Authorization who conducts appropriate inspections.", "certificated mechanic who maintains the aircraft."], "correct": 0, "short": "aircraft owner or operator.", "expl": "Primary responsibility for AD compliance rests with the aircraft owner or operator — they own the airworthiness of the aircraft. The mechanic performs and records the AD work, but the legal duty to see that ADs get done is the owner/operator's.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-26 (14 CFR 39.7 / 91.403)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8495", "q": "An aircraft Type Certificate Data Sheet contains", "options": ["maximum fuel grade to be used.", "location of the datum.", "control surface adjustment points."], "correct": 1, "short": "location of the datum.", "expl": "The Type Certificate Data Sheet is the formal description of the certificated product, and among the items it lists is the location of the datum — the reference point for weight-and-balance measurements. Max fuel grade and control-rigging points live in other documents/the TCDS depending on model, but the datum is a TCDS staple.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8496", "q": "Suitability for use of a specific propeller with a particular engine-airplane combination can be determined by reference to what informational source?", "options": ["Propeller Specifications or Propeller Type Certificate Data Sheet.", "Alphabetical Index of Current Propeller Type Certificate Data Sheets, Specifications, and Listings.", "Aircraft Specifications or Aircraft Type Certificate Data Sheet."], "correct": 2, "short": "Aircraft Specifications or Aircraft Type Certificate Data Sheet.", "expl": "To confirm a specific propeller is suitable for a given engine-airplane combination, look in the Aircraft Specifications or Aircraft Type Certificate Data Sheet — that's where the eligible propeller installations for the aircraft are listed. The propeller's own TCDS describes the prop, but the aircraft TCDS tells you what fits the aircraft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8497", "q": "When an aircraft is sold domestically, the Airworthiness Certificate", "options": ["is transferred with the aircraft at the time of sale.", "must be surrendered to the local Flight Standards District Office.", "becomes invalid until the new owner makes application for a new Airworthiness Certificate."], "correct": 0, "short": "is transferred with the aircraft at the time of sale.", "expl": "When an aircraft is sold domestically, its Airworthiness Certificate transfers with the aircraft at the time of sale. The certificate stays valid as long as the aircraft is maintained airworthy; it isn't surrendered or invalidated just because ownership changed.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30 (14 CFR 21.181)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8498", "q": "The issuance of an Airworthiness Certificate is governed by", "options": ["14 CFR Part 23.", "14 CFR Part 21.", "14 CFR Part 39."], "correct": 1, "short": "14 CFR Part 21.", "expl": "Issuance of Airworthiness Certificates is governed by 14 CFR Part 21 (Certification Procedures for Products and Parts). Part 23 is airworthiness standards for the airplane, and Part 39 is Airworthiness Directives — neither issues the certificate.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-4 (14 CFR Part 21)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8499", "q": "Specifications pertaining to an aircraft model manufactured under a type certificate of which less than 50 are shown on the FAA Aircraft Registry, can be found in the", "options": ["Summary of Discontinued Aircraft Specifications.", "FAA Statistical Handbook of Civil Aircraft Specifications.", "Aircraft Listing."], "correct": 2, "short": "Aircraft Listing.", "expl": "When fewer than 50 aircraft of a type-certificated model remain on the FAA registry, its specification data is carried in the Aircraft Listing rather than in the full Aircraft Specifications. So low-population models are found in the Aircraft Listing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-27 (Aircraft Specifications & Listings)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8500", "q": "Where are technical descriptions of certificated propellers found?", "options": ["Propeller Type Certificate Data Sheets.", "Applicable Airworthiness Directives.", "Aircraft Specifications."], "correct": 0, "short": "Propeller Type Certificate Data Sheets.", "expl": "Technical descriptions of certificated propellers are found in the Propeller Type Certificate Data Sheets — the TCDS is the formal description of the propeller. ADs correct unsafe conditions and aircraft specs cover the aircraft, but the prop's own TCDS describes the prop.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8501", "q": "What information is generally contained in Aircraft Specifications or Type Certificate Data Sheets?", "options": ["Empty weight of the aircraft.", "Control surface movements.", "Useful load of aircraft."], "correct": 1, "short": "Control surface movements.", "expl": "Aircraft Specifications and Type Certificate Data Sheets generally include control surface movements (travel limits) among the certification data. Empty weight and useful load are weight-and-balance record items specific to the individual aircraft, not general TCDS content.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8503", "q": "Technical information about older aircraft models, of which no more than 50 remain in service, can be found in the", "options": ["Summary of Deleted and Discontinued Aircraft Specifications.", "Index of Antique Aircraft.", "Aircraft Listing."], "correct": 2, "short": "Aircraft Listing.", "expl": "Technical information on older, low-population models — no more than 50 remaining in service — is found in the Aircraft Listing. It's the catch-all for models too few to warrant full current Aircraft Specifications.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-27 (Aircraft Specifications & Listings)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8503-1", "q": "Which of the following are sometimes used as authorization to deviate from an aircraft’s original type design?", "options": ["FAA Form 337, Supplemental Type Certificate, and Airworthiness Directive.", "FAA Form 337, Supplemental Type Certificate, and Technical Standard Order.", "Supplemental Type Certificate, Airworthiness Directive, and Technical Standard Order."], "correct": 0, "short": "FAA Form 337, Supplemental Type Certificate, and Airworthiness Directive.", "expl": "Authorizations sometimes used to deviate from an aircraft's original type design are the FAA Form 337 (approved major alteration), the Supplemental Type Certificate, and the Airworthiness Directive (which can mandate a design change). A TSO is a part-performance standard, not a design-change authorization for a particular aircraft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR Part 21)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8504", "q": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": ["The Federal Aviation Regulations do not require approval after compliance with the data of a Supplemental Type Certificate.", "The Federal Aviation Regulations require approval after compliance with the data of a Supplemental Type Certificate.", "An installation of an item manufactured in accordance with the Technical Standard Order system requires no further approval for installation in a particular aircraft."], "correct": 1, "short": "The Federal Aviation Regulations require approval after compliance with the data of a Supplemental Type Certificate.", "expl": "The regulations DO require FAA approval after compliance with STC data — installing per an STC is an alteration that must be approved for return to service (typically documented on Form 337). Following the STC isn't automatically self-approving.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-27 (14 CFR Part 21)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8507", "q": "An aircraft mechanic is privileged to perform major alterations on U.S. certificated aircraft; however, the work must be done in accordance with FAA-approved technical data before the aircraft can be returned to service. Which is NOT approved data?", "options": ["Airworthiness Directives.", "Supplemental Type Certificates.", "AC 43.13-2."], "correct": 2, "short": "AC 43.13-2.", "expl": "Airworthiness Directives and Supplemental Type Certificates ARE approved data. AC 43.13-2 is only ACCEPTABLE data (a source of acceptable methods) — it is NOT, by itself, approved data for a major alteration, so it's the odd one out here.", "cite": "AC 43.13-1B, and 14 CFR 43.13", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8509", "q": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": ["FAA publications such as Technical Standard Orders, Airworthiness Directives, Type Certificate Data Sheets, and Aircraft Specifications and Supplemental Type Certificates are all approved data.", "Manufacturer’s data and FAA publications such as Airworthiness Directives, Type Certificate Data Sheets, and advisory circulars are all approved data.", "FAA publications such as Airworthiness Directives, Type Certificate Data Sheets, and advisory circulars are all approved data."], "correct": 0, "short": "FAA publications such as Technical Standard Orders, Airworthiness Directives, Type Certificate Data Sheets, and Aircraft Specifications and Supplemental Type Certificates are all approved data.", "expl": "The all-approved-data list is the one containing only regulatory/design documents: Technical Standard Orders, Airworthiness Directives, Type Certificate Data Sheets, Aircraft Specifications, and Supplemental Type Certificates. Any list that throws advisory circulars in is wrong, because ACs are acceptable — not approved — data.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.13)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8509-1", "q": "A Technical Standard Order (TSO) is issued by whom?", "options": ["The Aircraft industry.", "The Administrator.", "Part manufacturers."], "correct": 1, "short": "The Administrator.", "expl": "A Technical Standard Order is issued by the Administrator (the FAA) — it's the FAA setting a minimum performance standard. Manufacturers apply for TSO authorization; they don't issue the TSO itself.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-4 (14 CFR Part 21, Subpart O)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8510", "q": "The Air Transport Association of America (ATA) Specification No. 100", "options": ["combines the aircraft into numbered systems and subsystems to simplify locating maintenance instructions.", "establishes a standard for the presentation of aircraft specifications in pilot operating handbooks.", "establishes a standard for the presentation of technical data in maintenance manuals."], "correct": 2, "short": "establishes a standard for the presentation of technical data in maintenance manuals.", "expl": "The ATA Specification No. 100 established the standard for how technical data is presented in maintenance manuals (the ATA chapter numbering system), so any manufacturer's manual is laid out the same way. It's about presentation/format of maintenance data, and it's the forerunner of today's iSpec 2200.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8511", "q": "Aviation Maintenance Alerts", "options": ["provide information about aircraft problems and suggested corrective actions.", "provide mandatory procedures to prevent or correct serious aircraft problems.", "provide temporary emergency procedures until Airworthiness Directives can be issued."], "correct": 0, "short": "provide information about aircraft problems and suggested corrective actions.", "expl": "Aviation Maintenance Alerts provide information about aircraft service problems and suggested corrective actions gathered from the field. They are advisory — informational only — not mandatory like an AD and not emergency directives.", "cite": "FAA AC 43-16, Aviation Maintenance Alerts", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8515", "q": "(Refer to Figure 63.) An aircraft has a total time in service of 468 hours. The Airworthiness Directive given was initially complied with at 454 hours in service. How many additional hours in service may be accumulated before the Airworthiness Directive must again be complied with?", "options": ["46.", "186.", "200."], "correct": 1, "short": "186.", "expl": "Read the AD's recurring interval off the record: it was first complied with at 454 hours and repeats every 200 hours, so it's next due at 454 + 200 = 654 hours. With the aircraft now at 468 hours, that leaves 654 - 468 = 186 additional hours before it must be complied with again.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-26", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": "63"}, {"id": "8515-1", "q": "The following words are an example of what kind of statement in an AD? “Required within the next 25 hours time-in-service after the effective date of this AD, unless already accomplished.”", "options": ["Amendment.", "Applicability.", "Compliance."], "correct": 2, "short": "Compliance.", "expl": "Wording like 'required within the next 25 hours time-in-service after the effective date, unless already accomplished' is the AD's COMPLIANCE statement — it tells you WHEN and under what conditions the action must be done. Applicability says WHICH products; the amendment number identifies the AD.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-26", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8515-2", "q": "The action required by an AD may take which the following forms?", "options": ["Inspection, part(s) replacement, design modification, and/or change in operating procedure(s).", "Inspection, part(s) replacement, design modification, and/or overall change in the content, form, and disposition of aircraft maintenance records.", "Inspection, part(s) replacement, design modification, change in operating procedure(s), and/or overall change in the content, form and disposition of aircraft maintenance records."], "correct": 0, "short": "Inspection, part(s) replacement, design modification, and/or change in operating procedure(s).", "expl": "An AD can require any combination of: inspection, part(s) replacement, design modification, and/or a change in operating procedure(s). What an AD does NOT do is dictate the content, form, or disposition of your maintenance records — that's governed by other regulations.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-26", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8515-3", "q": "Type Certificate Data Sheets are issued for which of the following products?", "options": ["Aircraft, engines, and appliances.", "Aircraft, engines, and propellers.", "Aircraft, engines, propellers, and appliances."], "correct": 1, "short": "Aircraft, engines, and propellers.", "expl": "Type Certificate Data Sheets are issued for aircraft, engines, and propellers — the three type-certificated products. Appliances are handled under TSOs and other approvals, not their own TCDS.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8516-1", "q": "When is a mechanic responsible for checking AD compliance?", "options": ["Never, the owner or operator is solely responsible.", "Anytime an aircraft or portion thereof is returned to service.", "When performing an inspection required under part 91, 125, or 135."], "correct": 2, "short": "When performing an inspection required under part 91, 125, or 135.", "expl": "A mechanic becomes responsible for checking AD compliance when performing an inspection required under Part 91, 125, or 135 — the inspection has to determine that applicable ADs are complied with. It isn't 'never' (the owner isn't solely responsible), and it isn't every single return to service.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-26 (14 CFR 43.15)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8516-3", "q": "What does the Type Certificate Data Sheet designation code “2 PCSM” mean? (K01)", "options": ["Two place (number of seats), closed, sea, monoplane.", "Two wing (biplane), primary category, semimonocoque (airframe).", "Neither of the other two choices."], "correct": 0, "short": "Two place (number of seats), closed, sea, monoplane.", "expl": "The TCDS/specification designation '2 PCSM' decodes as two-place (number of seats), closed, sea, monoplane — a shorthand for the aircraft's configuration. Learn the letter groups and the code reads itself.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-27 (Aircraft Specifications)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8517", "q": "A complete detailed inspection and adjustment of the valve mechanism will be made at the first 25 hours after the engine has been placed in service. Subsequent inspections of the valve mechanism will be made each second 50-hour period. From the above statement, at what intervals will valve mechanism inspections be performed?", "options": ["50 hours.", "100 hours.", "125 hours."], "correct": 1, "short": "100 hours.", "expl": "Translate the schedule: first inspection at 25 hours, then one at each SECOND 50-hour period means every 100 hours thereafter. So after the initial 25-hour check, valve-mechanism inspections fall at 100-hour intervals.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30 (ICA / maintenance schedule)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8518", "q": "Check thrust bearing nuts for tightness on new or newly overhauled engines at the first 50-hour inspection following installation. Subsequent inspections on thrust bearing nuts will be made at each third 50-hour inspection. From the above statement, at what intervals should an aviation mechanic check the thrust bearing nut for tightness?", "options": ["200 hours.", "250 hours.", "150 hours."], "correct": 2, "short": "150 hours.", "expl": "Work the wording: first check at 50 hours, then at each THIRD 50-hour inspection means every 150 hours after that. So the thrust-bearing-nut check recurs at 150-hour intervals.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-30 (ICA / maintenance schedule)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8531-8", "q": "Which of the following is an instance when a special flight permit may not be issued?", "options": ["Flying passengers on a scheduled operation.", "Flying the aircraft to a location where repairs or maintenance can be performed.", "Flying a customer demonstration flight."], "correct": 0, "short": "Flying passengers on a scheduled operation.", "expl": "A special flight permit (ferry permit) is for moving an aircraft that can fly safely but doesn't currently meet all airworthiness requirements — to a place for repairs, for a demonstration flight, etc. It may NOT be issued to fly paying passengers on a scheduled operation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-21 (14 CFR 21.197)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}, {"id": "8534", "q": "Which of these publications contains standards for protrusion of bolts, studs, and screws through self-locking nuts?", "options": ["AC 43.13-2.", "AC 43.13-1.", "Aircraft Specifications or Type Certificate Data Sheets."], "correct": 1, "short": "AC 43.13-1.", "expl": "Standards for how far a bolt, stud, or screw must protrude through a self-locking nut are found in AC 43.13-1B — the acceptable-methods handbook for hardware installation. AC 43.13-2 covers alterations, and specs/TCDS don't get into fastener protrusion.", "cite": "AC 43.13-1B, Ch. 7 (self-locking nuts)", "topicName": "Airworthiness Directives, TCDS & Approved Data", "fig": null}]}, {"code": "REPAIR", "title": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "ref": null, "qs": [{"id": "8215-3", "q": "When installing a castle nut, and the nut castellation does not align with the cotter pin hole in the bolt within the torque range, the acceptable practice is to", "options": ["change the nut or washer and try again.", "back the nut off and align it to the nearest hole.", "continue tightening the nut to the next hole."], "correct": 0, "short": "change the nut or washer and try again.", "expl": "When a castle nut's slots won't line up with the cotter-pin hole within the allowed torque range, you do NOT back off below minimum and you do NOT crank past maximum torque to reach the next slot. The correct fix is to change the washer or the nut and try again so alignment falls inside the proper torque range.", "cite": "AC 43.13-1B, ¶7-40 (Torque)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": null}, {"id": "8447", "q": "Which is an appliance major repair?", "options": ["Repairs to a propeller governor or its control.", "Overhaul of a hydraulic pressure pump.", "Troubleshooting and repairing broken circuits in landing light circuits."], "correct": 1, "short": "Overhaul of a hydraulic pressure pump.", "expl": "Overhaul of a hydraulic pressure pump is an appliance major repair under Part 43, Appendix A — appliance major repairs specifically include overhaul work. Repairing a governor control or troubleshooting a landing-light circuit are lesser tasks, not appliance major repairs.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-16 (14 CFR Part 43, App. A)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": null}, {"id": "8449", "q": "Which maintenance action is an airframe major repair?", "options": ["Changes to the wing or to fixed or movable control surfaces which affect flutter and vibration characteristics.", "Rewinding the field coil of an electrical accessory.", "The repair of portions of skin sheets by making additional seams."], "correct": 2, "short": "The repair of portions of skin sheets by making additional seams.", "expl": "Repairing portions of skin sheets by making additional seams is an airframe major REPAIR. Changing a wing or control surface in a way that affects flutter/vibration is a major ALTERATION (different category), and rewinding a field coil is an appliance job — so the additional-seams skin repair is the airframe major repair.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-16 (14 CFR Part 43, App. A)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": null}, {"id": "8512", "q": "(Refer to Figure 62, 62A, & 62B as necessary.) Which doubler(s) require(s) heat treatment before installation?", "options": ["-102.", "-101.", "Both."], "correct": 0, "short": "-102.", "expl": "Read the drawing's material/process notes for each part number: the -102 doubler is the one called out for heat treatment before installation. Match the dash number to its note block rather than assuming both parts get the same treatment.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, Ch. 3 (Aircraft Drawings — notes)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": "62"}, {"id": "8512-1", "q": "(Refer to Figures 62, 62A, and 62B as necessary.) How many parts will need to be fabricated by the mechanic in the construction and installation of one doubler?", "options": ["2.", "3.", "4."], "correct": 1, "short": "3.", "expl": "Counting from the drawing, building and installing one doubler requires the mechanic to fabricate 3 parts (the doubler plus its associated fabricated pieces shown in the parts list). Tally exactly what the drawing lists as fabricated, not purchased/standard, items.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, Ch. 3 (Aircraft Drawings — parts list)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": "62"}, {"id": "8513", "q": "(Refer to Figure 62, 62A, & 62B as necessary.) Using only the information given (when bend allowance, set back, etc. have been calculated) which doubler is it possible to construct and install?", "options": ["-102.", "Both.", "-101."], "correct": 2, "short": "-101.", "expl": "With only the information given, the -101 doubler is the one you can actually lay out, form, and install — its drawing supplies the needed dimensions. The -102 is missing data (or needs the heat-treat step) that keeps you from completing it from the sheet alone.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, Ch. 3 (Aircraft Drawings — dimensions)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": "62"}, {"id": "8514", "q": "(Refer to Figure 62.) The -100 in the title block (Area 1) is applicable to which doubler part number(s)?", "options": ["-101.", "-102.", "Both."], "correct": 0, "short": "-101.", "expl": "The -100 shown in the title block (Area 1) is the base/assembly number, and here it applies to the -101 doubler part number. Trace the dash-number family: the title-block number ties to the specific detail it details out.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, Ch. 3 (Aircraft Drawings — title block)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": "62"}, {"id": "8516-4", "q": "Which of the following includes all the regulatory definitions of “maintenance”?", "options": ["Overhaul, repair, parts replacement, and preservation, and preventive maintenance.", "Overhaul, repair, parts replacement, inspection, and preservation.", "Overhaul, repair, parts replacement, preservation, inspection, and preventive maintenance."], "correct": 1, "short": "Overhaul, repair, parts replacement, inspection, and preservation.", "expl": "The regulatory definition of maintenance is inspection, overhaul, repair, preservation, and the replacement of parts — but it does NOT include preventive maintenance (that's a separate category). The complete list is overhaul, repair, parts replacement, inspection, and preservation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-5 (14 CFR 1.1)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": null}, {"id": "8520", "q": "A repair, as performed on an airframe, shall mean", "options": ["the upkeep and preservation of the airframe including the component parts thereof.", "simple or minor preservation operations and the replacement of small standard parts not involving complex assembly operations.", "the restoration of the airframe to a condition for safe operation after damage or deterioration."], "correct": 2, "short": "the restoration of the airframe to a condition for safe operation after damage or deterioration.", "expl": "A repair, as applied to an airframe, means the restoration of the airframe to a condition for safe operation after damage or deterioration. Simple upkeep/preservation and replacing small standard parts fall under preventive maintenance, not 'repair.'", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-16 (14 CFR 1.1)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": null}, {"id": "8521", "q": "The replacement of fabric on fabric-covered parts such as wings, fuselages, stabilizers, or control surfaces is considered to be a", "options": ["major repair even though no other alteration or repair is performed.", "minor repair unless the new cover is different in any way from the original cover.", "minor repair unless the underlying structure is altered or repaired."], "correct": 0, "short": "major repair even though no other alteration or repair is performed.", "expl": "Replacing fabric on fabric-covered wings, fuselages, stabilizers, or control surfaces is classified as a major repair — even when nothing else is altered or repaired. The recover job by itself crosses the major-repair line.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-16 (14 CFR Part 43, App. A)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": null}, {"id": "8522", "q": "Which is classified as a major repair?", "options": ["Installation of new engine mounts obtained from the aircraft manufacturer.", "The splicing of skin sheets.", "Any repair of damaged stressed metal skin."], "correct": 1, "short": "The splicing of skin sheets.", "expl": "The splicing of skin sheets is a major repair. Installing manufacturer-supplied identical engine mounts, or an ordinary repair of stressed skin, doesn't automatically reach major-repair status — but splicing skin sheets does.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-16 (14 CFR Part 43, App. A)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": null}, {"id": "8527", "q": "The replacement of a damaged vertical stabilizer with a new identical stabilizer purchased from the aircraft manufacturer is considered a", "options": ["minor alteration.", "major repair.", "minor repair."], "correct": 2, "short": "minor repair.", "expl": "Replacing a damaged vertical stabilizer with a new, identical one bought from the aircraft manufacturer is a minor repair — you're restoring the aircraft with a like-for-like factory part, not altering the design or doing complex structural work.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-16 (14 CFR Part 43, App. A)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": null}, {"id": "8535", "q": "The replacement of a damaged engine mount with a new identical engine mount purchased from the aircraft manufacturer is considered a", "options": ["minor repair.", "major or minor repair, depending upon the complexity of the installation.", "major repair."], "correct": 0, "short": "minor repair.", "expl": "Swapping a damaged engine mount for a new, identical engine mount from the aircraft manufacturer is a minor repair. It's a straightforward like-for-like replacement of a standard part — no alteration and no complex assembly that would make it major.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-16 (14 CFR Part 43, App. A)", "topicName": "Repairs, Alterations & Their Classification", "fig": null}]}, {"code": "PRIV", "title": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "ref": null, "qs": [{"id": "8505", "q": "Which regulation provides information regarding instrument range markings for an airplane certificated in the normal category?", "options": ["14 CFR Part 23.", "14 CFR Part 21.", "14 CFR Part 25."], "correct": 0, "short": "14 CFR Part 23.", "expl": "Instrument range markings for a normal-category airplane are set by 14 CFR Part 23 (airworthiness standards for normal-category airplanes). Part 21 is certification procedures and Part 25 is transport-category — Part 23 is the one that governs the normal-category airplane's markings.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-4 (14 CFR Part 23)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8505-1", "q": "Which regulation provides the airworthiness standards for an airplane certificated in the normal category?", "options": ["14 CFR Part 27.", "14 CFR Part 23.", "14 CFR Part 25."], "correct": 1, "short": "14 CFR Part 23.", "expl": "The airworthiness standards for a normal-category airplane live in 14 CFR Part 23. Part 25 covers transport category and Part 27 covers normal-category rotorcraft, so for a normal-category airplane it's Part 23.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-4 (14 CFR Part 23)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8505-2", "q": "What regulations are covered by 14 CFR Part 1?", "options": ["Certification of airmen other than crewmembers.", "General operating and flight rules.", "Definitions and abbreviations."], "correct": 2, "short": "Definitions and abbreviations.", "expl": "14 CFR Part 1 is Definitions and Abbreviations — the dictionary for the whole regulation set. Certification of airmen is Part 65 and general operating rules are Part 91; Part 1 just defines the terms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-5 (14 CFR Part 1)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8506", "q": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": ["A certificated powerplant mechanic may make a minor repair on an aluminum propeller and approve for return to service.", "Propellers are NOT included in the Airworthiness Directive system.", "A certificated powerplant mechanic may make a major repair on an aluminum propeller and approve for return to service."], "correct": 0, "short": "A certificated powerplant mechanic may make a minor repair on an aluminum propeller and approve for return to service.", "expl": "A certificated powerplant mechanic may make a MINOR repair to an aluminum propeller and approve it for return to service, but not a major repair. Propeller major repairs are beyond the mechanic's approval authority — those go to a rated repair station or require additional approval.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.87)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8516-5", "q": "What is the maximum penalty for cheating or other unauthorized conduct when taking an FAA mechanic test?", "options": ["Ineligibility to receive any certificate or rating for one year.", "Ineligibility to receive any certificate or rating for one year, and suspension or revocation of any certificate held.", "Ineligibility to receive any certificate or rating for one year, and suspension of any certificate held."], "correct": 1, "short": "Ineligibility to receive any certificate or rating for one year, and suspension or revocation of any certificate held.", "expl": "Cheating or other unauthorized conduct on an FAA mechanic test carries a double penalty: ineligibility for any certificate or rating for one year AND suspension or revocation of any certificate already held. It's not just the one-year bar — your existing certificates are on the line too.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.18)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8519", "q": "Certificated mechanics with a powerplant rating may perform", "options": ["any inspection required by the Federal Aviation Regulations on a powerplant or propeller or any component thereof, and may release the same to service.", "100-hour and/or annual inspections required by the Federal Aviation Regulations on powerplants, propellers, or any components thereof, and may release the same to service.", "100-hour inspections required by the Federal Aviation Regulations on a powerplant, propeller, or any component thereof, and may release the same to service."], "correct": 2, "short": "100-hour inspections required by the Federal Aviation Regulations on a powerplant, propeller, or any component thereof, and may release the same to service.", "expl": "A powerplant-rated mechanic may perform the 100-hour inspection on a powerplant, propeller, or component and release it to service. Not 'any inspection required by the regulations' (that overstates it) — the privilege is specifically the 100-hour inspection within the rating.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.85 / 65.87)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8520-1", "q": "Under the Federal Aviation Regulations, an aviation maintenance technician is required to perform maintenance on an aircraft so that it", "options": ["is at least equal to its original or properly altered condition.", "always meets its original type design.", "exceeds minimum standards with regard to aerodynamic function, structural strength, resistance to vibration, and other qualities affecting airworthiness."], "correct": 0, "short": "is at least equal to its original or properly altered condition.", "expl": "The standard is that maintenance must return the aircraft to a condition at least equal to its original or properly altered condition. You don't have to keep it exactly at original type design, and you're not required to exceed the standards — 'at least equal to original/properly altered' is the rule.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.13)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8523", "q": "The 100-hour inspection required by Federal Aviation Regulations for certain aircraft being operated for hire may be performed by", "options": ["persons working under the supervision of an appropriately rated mechanic, but the aircraft must be approved by the mechanic for return to service.", "appropriately rated mechanics and approved by them for return to service.", "appropriately rated mechanics only if they have an inspection authorization."], "correct": 1, "short": "appropriately rated mechanics and approved by them for return to service.", "expl": "The 100-hour inspection required for certain for-hire aircraft may be performed by appropriately rated mechanics, and they themselves approve it for return to service — no inspection authorization is needed for a 100-hour. A person merely working under supervision cannot sign off the inspection.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.85 / 43.7)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8524", "q": "A person working under the supervision of a certificated mechanic with an airframe and powerplant rating is not authorized to perform", "options": ["repair of a wing brace strut by welding.", "repair of an engine mount by riveting.", "a 100-hour inspection."], "correct": 2, "short": "a 100-hour inspection.", "expl": "A person working under the supervision of an A&P is allowed to do lots of hands-on work (welding a strut, riveting an engine mount) but is specifically NOT authorized to perform a required 100-hour inspection — an inspection can't be delegated to an unsupervised-signoff helper.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.85 / 43.3)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8525", "q": "Certificated mechanics, under their general certificate privileges, may", "options": ["perform 100-hour inspection of instruments.", "perform minor repairs to instruments.", "perform minor alterations to instruments."], "correct": 0, "short": "perform 100-hour inspection of instruments.", "expl": "Under general certificate privileges a mechanic may perform a 100-hour inspection that includes the instruments, but may NOT perform minor repairs or minor alterations to the instruments themselves — instrument internal work belongs to an approved instrument repair station. So the allowed action is the 100-hour inspection of instruments.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-22 (14 CFR 65.81)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8526", "q": "An Airworthiness Directive requires that a propeller be altered. Certificated mechanics could", "options": ["not perform the work because it is an alteration.", "perform and approve the work for return to service if it is a minor alteration.", "not perform the work because they are not allowed to perform and approve for return to service, repairs or alterations to propellers."], "correct": 1, "short": "perform and approve the work for return to service if it is a minor alteration.", "expl": "When an AD calls for a propeller alteration, a certificated mechanic may perform and approve the work for return to service IF it is a minor alteration. A major propeller alteration would be beyond the mechanic's authority, but a minor one is within it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.87)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8528", "q": "FAA certificated mechanics may", "options": ["approve for return to service a major repair for which they are rated.", "supervise and approve a 100-hour inspection.", "approve for return to service a minor alteration they have performed appropriate to the rating(s) they hold."], "correct": 2, "short": "approve for return to service a minor alteration they have performed appropriate to the rating(s) they hold.", "expl": "A certificated mechanic may approve for return to service a MINOR alteration they performed, appropriate to the rating(s) they hold. Approving a major repair or the entire aircraft after a 100-hour requires more than the base mechanic privilege, but a minor alteration they did themselves is squarely allowed.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-22 (14 CFR 65.81 / 65.85 / 65.87)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8529", "q": "A certificated mechanic with a powerplant rating may perform the", "options": ["100-hour inspection required by the Federal Aviation Regulations on a powerplant or any component thereof and approve and return the same to service.", "annual inspection required by the Federal Aviation Regulations on a powerplant or any component thereof and approve and return the same to service.", "100-hour inspection required by the Federal Aviation Regulations on an airframe, powerplant or any other component thereof and approve and return the same to service."], "correct": 0, "short": "100-hour inspection required by the Federal Aviation Regulations on a powerplant or any component thereof and approve and return the same to service.", "expl": "A powerplant-rated mechanic may perform the 100-hour inspection on a powerplant or its components and return it to service — but only the 100-hour, and only on the powerplant side (not the airframe, and not the annual, which needs an IA). So it's the 100-hour on the powerplant.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.87)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8530", "q": "What part of the Federal Aviation Regulations prescribes the requirements for issuing mechanic certificates and associated ratings and the general operating rules for the holders of these certificates and ratings?", "options": ["14 CFR Part 43.", "14 CFR Part 65.", "14 CFR Part 91."], "correct": 1, "short": "14 CFR Part 65.", "expl": "14 CFR Part 65 prescribes the requirements for issuing mechanic certificates and ratings AND the general operating rules for the holders. Part 43 is maintenance rules and Part 91 is operating rules for aircraft — Part 65 is the mechanic's own certification part.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-4 (14 CFR Part 65)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8530-1", "q": "14 CFR Part 65 contains regulations regarding the certification of", "options": ["pilots, flight Instructors, and ground instructors.", "flight crewmembers other than pilots.", "airmen other than flight crewmembers."], "correct": 2, "short": "airmen other than flight crewmembers.", "expl": "Part 65 covers certification of airmen OTHER than flight crewmembers — mechanics, repairmen, parachute riggers, dispatchers, and the like. Pilots and flight crew are certificated under other parts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-4 (14 CFR Part 65)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8530-2", "q": "14 CFR Part 65 Subpart D contains regulations regarding the certification of", "options": ["mechanics.", "repairmen.", "pilots."], "correct": 0, "short": "mechanics.", "expl": "Within Part 65, Subpart D is specifically the certification of mechanics. (Repairmen are Subpart E.) So Subpart D is the mechanics' subpart.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-4 (14 CFR 65, Subpart D)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8531", "q": "A certificated mechanic shall not exercise the privileges of the certificate and rating unless, within the preceding 24 months, the Administrator has found that the certificate holder is able to do the work or the certificate holder has", "options": ["served as a mechanic under the certificate and rating for at least 18 months.", "served as a mechanic under the certificate and rating for at least 6 months.", "served as a mechanic under the certificate and rating for at least 12 months."], "correct": 1, "short": "served as a mechanic under the certificate and rating for at least 6 months.", "expl": "To keep exercising the privileges, within the preceding 24 months the mechanic must have either been found able by the Administrator OR have served as a mechanic under the certificate and rating for at least 6 months. Six months of recent experience is the qualifying threshold.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.83)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8531-1", "q": "Under Title 14 of the Code of Federal Regulations, what is the maximum penalty for falsification, alteration, or fraudulent reproduction of certificates, logbooks, reports, and records?", "options": ["Ineligibility to receive any certificate or rating for one year.", "Imprisonment for one year and a $5,000 fine.", "Suspension or revocation of any certificate held."], "correct": 2, "short": "Suspension or revocation of any certificate held.", "expl": "Falsification, alteration, or fraudulent reproduction of certificates, logbooks, reports, or records is a basis for suspension or revocation of any certificate held. It's a certificate-action penalty — the FAA can pull your ticket.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-17 (14 CFR 65.20)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8531-3", "q": "How long is a mechanic certificate with airframe and/or powerplant rating(s) valid?", "options": ["Until voluntarily surrendered, permanently revoked, or temporarily suspended.", "Until the holder is relieved of duties for which the holder was employed and certificated.", "24 months after the holder has last exercised the privileges of the certificate."], "correct": 0, "short": "Until voluntarily surrendered, permanently revoked, or temporarily suspended.", "expl": "A mechanic certificate with airframe and/or powerplant ratings has no expiration date — it stays valid until it is voluntarily surrendered, permanently revoked, or temporarily suspended. It doesn't lapse just because you stop working or 24 months pass (that only affects exercising privileges).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.15)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8531-4", "q": "Why is a mechanic applicant issued a temporary certificate after successful completion of the required tests?", "options": ["So that a background check/investigation may be completed.", "To allow for review of their application and supplementary documents.", "Both of the other two choices."], "correct": 1, "short": "To allow for review of their application and supplementary documents.", "expl": "A mechanic applicant gets a temporary certificate after passing the tests so they can work while the FAA reviews the application and supporting documents before issuing the permanent certificate. It's about processing the paperwork, not a background investigation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.19)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8531-5", "q": "What is the maximum duration of a temporary airman certificate?", "options": ["60 days.", "90 days.", "120 days."], "correct": 2, "short": "120 days.", "expl": "A temporary airman certificate is valid for a maximum of 120 days. That's the window you have to work under it while the permanent certificate is being issued.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.19)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8531-6", "q": "When may an otherwise qualified mechanic who does not read, write, speak, and understand the English language be eligible to apply for a mechanic certificate?", "options": ["When employed outside the United States by a U.S. air carrier.", "When a special authorization has been issued by the supervising FAA Flight Standards Office.", "When employed outside the United States."], "correct": 0, "short": "When employed outside the United States by a U.S. air carrier.", "expl": "An otherwise-qualified mechanic who does not read, write, speak, and understand English may still be eligible when employed OUTSIDE the United States by a U.S. air carrier — with the certificate limited to that employment. Inside the U.S., English proficiency is required.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.71)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8531-7", "q": "Which of the following statements is true for a certificated and appropriately rated mechanic regarding repairs and alterations?", "options": ["He or she may perform airframe minor repairs and minor alterations and approve the work for return to service, but cannot perform an airframe major repair or major alteration.", "He or she may perform an airframe major repair or major alteration, but cannot approve the work for return to service.", "He or she may perform an airframe major repair or major alteration and approve the work, but not the entire aircraft, for return to service."], "correct": 1, "short": "He or she may perform an airframe major repair or major alteration, but cannot approve the work for return to service.", "expl": "A certificated, appropriately rated mechanic MAY perform an airframe major repair or major alteration, but CANNOT approve that major work for return to service — that approval requires an Inspection Authorization. Perform yes, approve-the-major no.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.85 / 65.87)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8532", "q": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": ["Certificated mechanics with an airframe rating may perform a minor repair to an airspeed indicator providing they have the necessary equipment available.", "Certificated mechanics with a powerplant rating may perform a major repair to a propeller providing they have the necessary equipment available.", "Certificated mechanics with a powerplant rating may perform a minor repair to a propeller providing they have the necessary equipment available."], "correct": 2, "short": "Certificated mechanics with a powerplant rating may perform a minor repair to a propeller providing they have the necessary equipment available.", "expl": "A powerplant-rated mechanic may perform a MINOR repair to a propeller (provided they have the necessary equipment). A major propeller repair or airspeed-indicator internal work is outside the base mechanic privilege — but a minor prop repair is fine.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.87)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8533", "q": "Who is responsible for determining that materials used in aircraft maintenance and repair are of the proper type and conform to the appropriate standards?", "options": ["The installing person or agency.", "The owner or operator of the aircraft.", "The manufacturer of the aircraft."], "correct": 0, "short": "The installing person or agency.", "expl": "Responsibility for ensuring the materials used are the proper type and conform to the appropriate standards rests with the installing person or agency — the one actually doing the work. You can't push that off onto the owner or the manufacturer; if you install it, you own conformance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-15 (14 CFR 43.13)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8536", "q": "Who has the authority to approve a propeller for return to service after a 100-hour inspection?", "options": ["Any certificated repairman.", "A certificated mechanic with a powerplant rating.", "A non-certificated mechanic working under the supervision of a certificated mechanic with airframe and powerplant ratings."], "correct": 1, "short": "A certificated mechanic with a powerplant rating.", "expl": "Authority to approve a propeller for return to service after a 100-hour inspection belongs to a certificated mechanic holding a powerplant rating. A repairman or a supervised non-certificated helper can't sign that approval — it takes the powerplant rating.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-19 (14 CFR 65.87)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8537", "q": "Instrument repairs may be performed", "options": ["by the instrument manufacturer only.", "on airframe instruments by mechanics with an airframe rating.", "by an FAA-approved instrument repair station."], "correct": 2, "short": "by an FAA-approved instrument repair station.", "expl": "Instrument repairs are performed by an FAA-approved instrument repair station — not by an airframe mechanic and not only by the original manufacturer. Internal instrument work is specialized and requires that approved repair-station rating.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-22 (14 CFR 65.81)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}, {"id": "8598", "q": "Which of the following instrument discrepancies could be corrected by an aviation mechanic?", "options": ["Red line missing, mounting screws loose, case paint chipped, or leaking at line B nut.", "Case paint chipped, leaking at line B nut, mounting screws loose, or case leaking.", "Red line missing, case leaking, glass cracked, or fogged."], "correct": 0, "short": "Red line missing, mounting screws loose, case paint chipped, or leaking at line B nut.", "expl": "A mechanic can correct the external/mechanical instrument squawks — remarking a missing red line, tightening loose mounting screws, touching up chipped case paint, and fixing a leak at an external line nut. Internal faults like a fogged or cracked glass or a leaking case need an approved instrument repair station, so the all-external set is the one a mechanic may handle.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 2-22 (14 CFR 65.81)", "topicName": "Mechanic Privileges & Certification (Part 65)", "fig": null}]}, {"code": "HF", "title": "Human Factors", "ref": null, "qs": [{"id": "8538", "q": "How is a quality system assured?", "options": ["By an internal reporting and auditing system.", "By an independent organization.", "By a dependent organization."], "correct": 0, "short": "By an internal reporting and auditing system.", "expl": "A quality system is assured from the inside — through an internal reporting and auditing system that lets an organization catch and correct its own problems. It isn't guaranteed by an outside body; the organization polices itself through reporting and audits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 14-12", "topicName": "Human Factors", "fig": null}, {"id": "8539", "q": "According to Dr. James Reason, there are two types of human failure which can occur. They are:", "options": ["mental and physical.", "active and latent.", "proper and improper."], "correct": 1, "short": "active and latent.", "expl": "Dr. James Reason describes two kinds of human failure: active failures (errors with immediate effect, made by the person at the 'sharp end') and latent failures (hidden conditions built into the system that lie dormant until they contribute to an accident). So the pair is active and latent.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 14-2", "topicName": "Human Factors", "fig": null}, {"id": "8540", "q": "Many areas of aviation have shifted their focus from eliminating error to", "options": ["identifying and mitigating error.", "reducing and containing error.", "preventing and managing error."], "correct": 2, "short": "preventing and managing error.", "expl": "Aviation has moved away from trying to ELIMINATE error (impossible for humans) toward preventing and managing error — building systems that catch and trap mistakes before they cause harm. The modern goal is managing error, not pretending it can be erased.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 14-7", "topicName": "Human Factors", "fig": null}, {"id": "8541", "q": "When we think of aviation safety in a contemporary way, human error is", "options": ["the starting point.", "the ending point.", "the intervention point."], "correct": 0, "short": "the starting point.", "expl": "In the contemporary view of safety, human error is treated as the STARTING point of an investigation, not the conclusion — you begin with the error and dig into the conditions that produced it. Error is where the inquiry begins, not where it ends.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 14-7", "topicName": "Human Factors", "fig": null}, {"id": "8542", "q": "All of the following are consequences of human error, except", "options": ["catastrophic.", "mental stressor.", "personal injury."], "correct": 1, "short": "mental stressor.", "expl": "The consequences of human error include catastrophic outcomes and personal injury. A 'mental stressor' is a CONTRIBUTING factor that helps cause error — it's an input, not a consequence — so it's the item that doesn't belong on the consequences list.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 14-1", "topicName": "Human Factors", "fig": null}, {"id": "8543", "q": "The positive aspects of human factor issues are referred to as the", "options": ["Dirty Dozen.", "MEDA.", "Magnificent Seven."], "correct": 2, "short": "Magnificent Seven.", "expl": "The negatives are the famous 'Dirty Dozen'; the positive aspects of human-factors issues are referred to as the 'Magnificent Seven' — the good habits/traits that counter the twelve common error preconditions. So the positive counterpart is the Magnificent Seven.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 14-13", "topicName": "Human Factors", "fig": null}, {"id": "8544", "q": "The SHEL model is another human factors tool. The goal is to determine not only what the problem is, but also", "options": ["where and why it exists.", "how we prevent the problem.", "how many factors contribute to the error."], "correct": 0, "short": "where and why it exists.", "expl": "The SHEL model (Software, Hardware, Environment, Liveware) is used to determine not only WHAT the problem is but also WHERE and WHY it exists — it maps the person against the other elements to find where the mismatch occurred. Its goal is the where-and-why, not just the what.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 14-11", "topicName": "Human Factors", "fig": null}, {"id": "8545", "q": "The three types of human error are", "options": ["active, latent, and stressor.", "omission, commission, and extraneous.", "mental, situational, and physiological."], "correct": 1, "short": "omission, commission, and extraneous.", "expl": "The three types of human error are omission (leaving out a required task), commission (doing a task incorrectly), and extraneous (performing a task that wasn't authorized). Omission, commission, and extraneous — that's the set.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B, p. 14-1", "topicName": "Human Factors", "fig": null}]}]};
